/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/a-plus-core1.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("a-plus-core1", [["ap1",0,1,"A laptop's touchpad has started bulging upward and the lid no longer closes flat. What should the technician do first?",["Power it off, unplug it and remove the swollen battery","Fully discharge and recharge the battery to recalibrate it","Update the touchpad driver and the system firmware","Press the case closed and reseat the touchpad cable"],0,"A bulging touchpad usually means a swelling lithium-ion battery, which is a fire risk; stop charging it and replace it. Recalibrating a swollen battery keeps a dangerous part in service.","Objective 1.1",[null,"Charge cycling a swollen lithium-ion battery keeps a damaged, fire-prone cell in service and may make the swelling worse.","Drivers and firmware cannot fix a physical bulge; the touchpad is being pushed up by the battery underneath it.","Pressing on a swollen lithium-ion battery can puncture it and start a fire, and the cable is not the cause of the bulge."],2],["ap2",0,1,"A technician opens an ultrathin laptop to add memory but finds no SODIMM slots. What is the most likely explanation?",["The SODIMM slots sit behind the display panel","The slots stay hidden until enabled in UEFI","The memory is soldered onto the system board","The laptop takes full-size DIMMs under the keyboard"],2,"Many thin laptops use memory soldered to the motherboard, so it cannot be upgraded. Firmware settings never hide physical slots.","Objective 1.1",["Laptop memory is never mounted behind the display panel; the slots, when present, sit on the system board under the bottom cover or keyboard.","UEFI can disable features but cannot hide physical connectors; if no slot is visible on the board, none exists.",null,"Laptops use SODIMMs or soldered memory, not full-size desktop DIMMs, which would not fit in a thin chassis."],3],["ap3",0,1,"A laptop has an M.2 slot that supports PCIe. Which replacement drive gives the best storage performance?",["An M.2 SATA SSD","A 2.5-inch 7200 RPM hard drive","A 2.5-inch SATA SSD in an adapter","An M.2 NVMe SSD"],3,"NVMe drives use PCIe lanes and are much faster than anything limited to the SATA interface, including M.2 SATA SSDs that look similar.","Objective 1.1",["An M.2 SATA SSD uses the M.2 form factor but is still limited to the roughly 600 MB/s SATA interface, well below PCIe NVMe speeds.","A spinning 7200 RPM hard drive is by far the slowest option, with mechanical seek times, and would not even use the M.2 slot.","An adapter does not change the interface; a 2.5-inch SATA SSD is still capped by SATA bandwidth.",null],2],["ap4",0,1,"After replacing a laptop's Wi-Fi card, the user sees only one weak network even next to the access point. What was most likely missed?",["The new card needs a static IP address","The access point must be rebooted after a card swap","The SSID has to be re-entered in UEFI setup","The antenna leads were not reattached to the card"],3,"Laptop Wi-Fi antennas usually run through the display bezel and clip onto the card; without them the card barely receives. IP settings do not affect signal strength.","Objective 1.1",["IP addressing happens after association and has no effect on how many networks the card can hear or how strong the signal is.","Swapping the laptop's card does not require rebooting the access point, and a reboot would not improve the laptop's reception.","SSIDs are entered in the operating system's Wi-Fi settings, not in UEFI, and doing so would not fix weak reception.",null],3],["ap5",0,1,"A user wants to pair a new Bluetooth headset with a phone. Bluetooth is already on. What is the next step?",["Put the headset in pairing mode","Tap the headset against the phone's NFC area","Connect the headset to the phone with USB-C","Join the headset to the same Wi-Fi network"],0,"Pairing needs the accessory to be discoverable; then the user selects it on the phone and confirms the PIN or passkey if asked. Wi-Fi plays no part in Bluetooth pairing.","Objective 1.2",[null,"NFC tap-to-pair only works if both devices support it, and the headset still has to be discoverable; it is not the standard next step.","A USB-C cable charges the headset or carries wired audio but does not perform Bluetooth pairing.","Bluetooth pairing is a direct device-to-device link and has nothing to do with joining a Wi-Fi network."],2],["ap6",0,1,"Which wireless technology lets a phone make a payment when held within a few centimeters of a terminal?",["Bluetooth","Infrared","NFC","Wi-Fi Direct"],2,"Near-field communication works only over a few centimeters, which suits tap-to-pay. Bluetooth works over meters and is not used for contactless card payments.","Objective 1.2",["Bluetooth works over several meters and requires pairing, so it is not what contactless payment terminals use.","Infrared needs line of sight and is used for remotes and old data links, not modern tap-to-pay.",null,"Wi-Fi Direct connects devices peer-to-peer over tens of meters and is not used for contactless card payments."],1],["ap7",0,1,"A traveling employee needs internet on a laptop at a site with no Wi-Fi. The employee's phone has an unlimited cellular plan. What is the quickest fix?",["Enable NFC on both devices","Pair the laptop and phone for file transfer","Enable the phone's location services","Turn on the phone's mobile hotspot"],3,"A hotspot (tethering) shares the phone's cellular data with the laptop over Wi-Fi, USB or Bluetooth. File transfer pairing does not share an internet connection.","Objective 1.3",["NFC only exchanges small amounts of data over a few centimeters and cannot share an internet connection.","Pairing for file transfer creates a Bluetooth link for files but does not route the phone's cellular data to the laptop.","Location services only let apps know the phone's position; they do not provide network access to another device.",null],2],["ap8",0,1,"A company wants to push email settings to staff phones, require a screen lock and remotely wipe a lost device. What should it deploy?",["A site-to-site VPN","Mobile device management","A captive portal on the guest Wi-Fi","A KVM switch in each office"],1,"MDM enrolls devices so IT can push configuration, enforce policies such as passcodes and wipe lost devices. A VPN only protects traffic in transit.","Objective 1.3",["A site-to-site VPN connects two networks' routers and has no ability to configure, lock or wipe individual phones.",null,"A captive portal only shows a login or terms page before granting guest Wi-Fi access; it cannot manage devices.","A KVM switch shares one keyboard, video and mouse among several computers and has nothing to do with phones."],2],["ap9",0,1,"A user wants a single cable from a laptop to connect two monitors, wired Ethernet, a keyboard and charging. What should the technician recommend?",["A KVM switch","A USB-C or Thunderbolt dock","A PoE injector","A USB 2.0 hub"],1,"A USB-C/Thunderbolt docking station carries video, data, network and power delivery over one cable. A KVM shares one set of peripherals between several computers.","Objective 1.2",["A KVM switch lets one set of peripherals control several computers; it does not provide Ethernet, charging or multiple displays over one cable.",null,"A PoE injector adds power to an Ethernet cable for devices like cameras and access points, not video, peripherals and laptop charging.","A USB 2.0 hub only adds low-speed USB ports; it cannot drive two monitors or deliver laptop charging power."],2],["ap10",0,1,"Which phone component determines location from satellites even when no cellular or Wi-Fi signal is available?",["GPS receiver","Gyroscope","NFC controller","Digitizer"],0,"The GPS (GNSS) receiver calculates position from satellite signals. A gyroscope only measures rotation and orientation.","Objective 1.3",[null,"A gyroscope measures rotation and orientation, which helps with screen rotation and motion, but cannot determine location.","An NFC controller handles short-range tap communication such as payments and has no role in positioning.","The digitizer is the touch-sensing layer of the screen and has nothing to do with location."],1],["ap11",0,1,"A tablet's display shows a clear picture, but taps in the lower third of the screen are ignored. Which part most likely needs replacing?",["The digitizer","The LCD backlight","The display inverter","The graphics processor"],0,"The digitizer is the touch layer; a damaged one ignores touch while the image stays fine. A backlight fault would dim the picture, not block touch.","Objective 1.1",[null,"The picture is clear and bright, so the backlight is working; a backlight fault would dim the whole image, not block touch.","An inverter powers old CCFL backlights and affects brightness, not touch input; modern tablets use LED backlights without one.","A GPU fault would distort or blank the image, but the display here is fine and only touch input fails."],2],["ap12",0,1,"A graphic designer wants to draw with pressure sensitivity on a laptop that has no touchscreen. Which accessory fits?",["A webcam","A drawing pad","A trackpoint","A barcode scanner"],1,"A drawing pad (graphics tablet) with a stylus lets the user draw with pressure sensitivity on any laptop. A trackpoint only moves the pointer.","Objective 1.2",["A webcam captures video for calls and cannot be used as a pressure-sensitive drawing surface.",null,"A trackpoint is a small pointing stick for moving the cursor; it offers no pen input or pressure sensitivity.","A barcode scanner reads printed codes for inventory or retail and cannot be used for drawing."],2],["ap13",0,2,"A help desk needs to connect to users' Windows desktops with Remote Desktop through an internal firewall. Which port must be open?",["TCP 3389","TCP 22","TCP 445","UDP 3390"],0,"RDP listens on TCP 3389. Port 22 is SSH, which Windows Remote Desktop does not use.","Objective 2.1",[null,"TCP 22 is SSH, used for secure command-line access, not the Windows Remote Desktop Protocol.","TCP 445 carries SMB file and printer sharing, not Remote Desktop sessions.","RDP uses port 3389 (TCP, and optionally UDP 3389); 3390 is not the RDP port."],1],["ap14",0,2,"Users can reach a website by its IP address but not by its name. Traffic on which port is most likely being blocked?",["80","25","53","110"],2,"Name resolution uses DNS on port 53 (UDP and TCP). Port 80 carries HTTP, which works here because the site loads by IP.","Objective 2.1",["Port 80 is HTTP; it clearly works because the site loads when reached by its IP address.","Port 25 is SMTP for mail transfer between servers and plays no part in resolving website names.",null,"Port 110 is POP3 for downloading email and is unrelated to looking up host names."],2],["ap15",0,2,"Which statement best describes UDP?",["Connectionless, with no delivery guarantee","Connection-oriented, with a three-way handshake","Resends lost segments and reorders them","Used only for secure web traffic"],0,"UDP sends datagrams without a handshake or acknowledgments, which suits DNS queries, DHCP and streaming. The handshake and retransmission belong to TCP.","Objective 2.1",[null,"Connection-oriented delivery with a three-way handshake describes TCP, not UDP.","Retransmitting lost segments and putting them back in order are TCP features; UDP leaves that to the application if needed.","Secure web traffic is HTTPS, which mostly runs over TCP 443; UDP carries many kinds of traffic, such as DNS, DHCP and streaming."],1],["ap16",0,2,"A technician is troubleshooting Windows file shares that fail to open across the office. Which port does SMB use directly?",["TCP 389","TCP 445","UDP 161","TCP 143"],1,"Modern SMB runs directly over TCP 445. Port 389 is LDAP, which is used for directory lookups, not file sharing.","Objective 2.1",["TCP 389 is LDAP, used to query directory services such as Active Directory, not to carry file-share traffic.",null,"UDP 161 is SNMP, used to monitor and manage network devices, not Windows file sharing.","TCP 143 is IMAP, used by email clients to read mail on a server."],1],["ap17",0,2,"Which protocol and port do mail servers use to send email to other mail servers?",["IMAP on port 143","POP3 on port 110","SMTP on port 25","LDAP on port 389"],2,"SMTP (port 25) transfers mail between servers. IMAP and POP3 are used by clients to retrieve mail from their own server.","Objective 2.1",["IMAP on 143 lets email clients read and manage mail stored on their own server; it does not send mail between servers.","POP3 on 110 lets clients download mail from their mailbox; it is not used for server-to-server delivery.",null,"LDAP on 389 queries directory services such as address books; it does not transfer email."],1],["ap18",0,2,"A small office replaces an old hub with a device that learns MAC addresses and forwards frames only to the destination port. What was installed?",["A repeater","A modem","A switch","A patch panel"],2,"A switch keeps a MAC address table and forwards frames only where they need to go. A hub or repeater sends every frame out every port.","Objective 2.2",["A repeater simply regenerates the signal and sends it on, with no knowledge of MAC addresses.","A modem converts signals between the ISP's medium (cable, DSL) and Ethernet; it does not forward frames by MAC address.",null,"A patch panel is a passive termination point for cable runs and makes no forwarding decisions."],1],["ap19",0,2,"New IP cameras must be mounted on a warehouse ceiling where there are no power outlets. Which switch feature should be used?",["Power over Ethernet","Port mirroring","Link aggregation","Jumbo frames"],0,"PoE carries power and data over the same twisted-pair cable to cameras, phones and access points. Port mirroring only copies traffic for analysis.","Objective 2.2",[null,"Port mirroring copies traffic from one port to another for monitoring; it supplies no power.","Link aggregation bundles several ports for more bandwidth or redundancy but does not power devices.","Jumbo frames allow larger Ethernet frames for efficiency on storage networks; they have nothing to do with power."],2],["ap20",0,2,"A technician is setting up a 2.4 GHz network for an office with three access points. Which channels avoid overlap?",["1, 6 and 11","1, 2 and 3","3, 7 and 9","4, 8 and 12"],0,"In the 2.4 GHz band only channels 1, 6 and 11 do not overlap in North America. Neighboring channel numbers share most of their frequencies.","Objective 2.3",[null,"Channels 1, 2 and 3 are only 5 MHz apart, so their roughly 20 MHz-wide signals overlap heavily.","Channels 3, 7 and 9 are too close together; 7 and 9 in particular overlap almost completely.","Channels 4, 8 and 12 are only four channels apart, so they still overlap, and channel 12 is not allowed in North America."],2],["ap21",0,2,"Which Wi-Fi generation matches the IEEE 802.11ax standard?",["Wi-Fi 6","Wi-Fi 4","Wi-Fi 5","Wi-Fi 3"],0,"802.11ax is Wi-Fi 6 (and Wi-Fi 6E when it uses 6 GHz). 802.11ac is Wi-Fi 5 and 802.11n is Wi-Fi 4.","Objective 2.3",[null,"Wi-Fi 4 is 802.11n, the earlier standard that introduced MIMO on 2.4 and 5 GHz.","Wi-Fi 5 is 802.11ac, which operates on 5 GHz and came before 802.11ax.","Wi-Fi 3 is the retroactive name for 802.11g, a 2.4 GHz standard much older than 802.11ax."],1],["ap22",0,2,"A home office owner wants the strongest security available on a new wireless router that all devices support. What should be chosen?",["WEP with a long key","WPA with TKIP","WPA3-Personal","An open network with MAC filtering"],2,"WPA3-Personal uses SAE, which resists offline password guessing. WEP and TKIP are broken, and MAC filtering is easy to bypass.","Objective 2.5",["WEP is broken regardless of key length and can be cracked in minutes by capturing traffic.","The original WPA with TKIP has known weaknesses and is deprecated; it is far weaker than WPA3.",null,"An open network leaves traffic unencrypted, and MAC filtering is trivially bypassed by spoofing an allowed address."],2],["ap23",0,2,"A company wants employees to log in to Wi-Fi with their own domain credentials instead of a shared passphrase. Which server does the access point need to use?",["A syslog server","A RADIUS server","A proxy server","A DHCP server"],1,"WPA2/WPA3-Enterprise hands authentication to a RADIUS (AAA) server that checks each user's credentials. A DHCP server only hands out addresses.","Objective 2.4",["A syslog server collects log messages from devices; it does not authenticate users.",null,"A proxy server forwards and filters client web requests; it does not verify Wi-Fi logins.","A DHCP server only assigns IP addresses after a device has joined; it performs no user authentication."],2],["ap24",0,2,"A network printer's address keeps changing, which breaks users' print mappings. What is the best fix on a SOHO router?",["Shorten the DHCP lease time","Enable UPnP on the router","Turn on the router's DMZ","Create a DHCP reservation for its MAC"],3,"A reservation always gives the printer the same address while keeping central management. Shorter leases would make the address change even more often.","Objective 2.6",["A shorter lease makes clients renew more often and increases the chance the printer receives a different address.","UPnP lets devices open ports on the router automatically; it does not keep a device's IP address fixed and is a security risk.","A DMZ exposes one host to all inbound internet traffic, which is risky and does nothing to stop the address from changing.",null],3],["ap25",0,2,"A user hosts a game server on a home PC and friends cannot connect from the internet. What should be configured on the SOHO router?",["A new SSID on the 5 GHz band","Port forwarding to the PC's private IP","MAC filtering for the PC","A static DNS entry for the router"],1,"Port forwarding sends incoming traffic on a given port through NAT to one internal host. A new SSID does nothing for inbound internet traffic.","Objective 2.5",["A new SSID only affects local wireless clients and does nothing to let internet traffic reach the PC through NAT.",null,"MAC filtering controls which local devices may join the network; it does not allow inbound connections from the internet.","A static DNS entry changes how names resolve and would not route inbound game traffic through NAT to the PC."],2],["ap26",0,2,"Which address is a private IPv4 address that will not be routed on the public internet?",["172.34.1.10","172.20.5.4","11.0.0.5","193.168.1.20"],1,"Private ranges are 10.0.0.0/8, 172.16.0.0–172.31.255.255 and 192.168.0.0/16. 172.34.1.10 is outside 172.16–31, so it is public.","Objective 2.6",["172.34.1.10 is outside the private 172.16.0.0 to 172.31.255.255 range, so it is a public, routable address.",null,"11.0.0.5 is public; only 10.0.0.0/8 is private, so a first octet of 11 is not in any private range.","193.168.1.20 looks like 192.168 but is a public address; only 192.168.0.0/16 is private."],1],["ap27",0,2,"A mail administrator wants to publish which servers may send email for the company's domain so receivers can reject forged mail. Which DNS record holds this?",["An MX record","A CNAME record","An AAAA record","A TXT record containing SPF"],3,"SPF is published in a TXT record and lists allowed sending servers; DKIM and DMARC also use TXT records. MX records only say where to deliver incoming mail.","Objective 2.6",["An MX record tells senders which servers accept incoming mail for the domain; it does not authorize outgoing senders.","A CNAME record makes one name an alias of another name and holds no sender policy.","An AAAA record maps a host name to an IPv6 address and cannot list authorized mail senders.",null],3],["ap28",0,2,"A company wants guest devices and accounting PCs on the same physical switches but kept on separate networks. What should be configured?",["VLANs","A VPN","A proxy server","NAT"],0,"VLANs split one switch into separate broadcast domains so traffic stays apart unless routed. A VPN encrypts traffic over untrusted networks instead.","Objective 2.6",[null,"A VPN creates an encrypted tunnel over an untrusted network; it does not separate devices on the same local switches.","A proxy server relays and filters client web requests but does not split a switch into separate networks.","NAT translates private addresses to public ones at the network edge; it does not isolate groups of devices on internal switches."],2],["ap29",0,2,"A remote cabin can only get geostationary satellite internet. Which issue should the owner expect most?",["High latency for video calls and gaming","No support for web browsing","Frequent DHCP address conflicts","Crosstalk on the indoor cabling"],0,"Signals must travel to a satellite about 36,000 km up and back, adding hundreds of milliseconds of delay. Browsing still works, just with lag.","Objective 2.7",[null,"Satellite internet supports ordinary web browsing; pages just load with noticeable delay.","DHCP address conflicts are a local network issue and are not caused by the type of internet connection.","Crosstalk is interference between wire pairs in copper cabling and has nothing to do with satellite service."],3],["ap30",0,2,"A user connects wireless earbuds and a smartwatch to a phone. Which network type do these devices form?",["LAN","PAN","MAN","SAN"],1,"A personal area network links devices a few meters from one person, usually over Bluetooth. A SAN is dedicated storage networking.","Objective 2.7",["A LAN connects computers across a building or office, a much larger scope than devices worn by one person.",null,"A MAN spans a city or campus, far larger than a few devices around one user.","A SAN is a dedicated high-speed network connecting servers to shared storage, not wearable devices."],1],["ap31",0,2,"A technician is terminating new Cat 6 runs onto a 110 block in the wiring closet. Which tool is needed?",["A punchdown tool","An RJ45 crimping tool","A loopback plug","A toner probe"],0,"A punchdown tool seats wires into 110 or Krone blocks and trims them. A crimper is for attaching RJ45 plugs to patch cables.","Objective 2.8",[null,"An RJ45 crimper attaches modular plugs to patch-cable ends; it cannot seat wires into a 110 block.","A loopback plug tests a port by sending its signal back to itself; it does not terminate cable.","A toner probe traces and identifies cables but is not used to terminate them."],2],["ap32",0,2,"In a closet full of unlabeled cables, a technician must find which one runs to office 214's wall jack. Which tool helps most?",["A Wi-Fi analyzer","A digital multimeter","A cable stripper","A toner and probe"],3,"The toner puts a signal on the cable at the jack, and the probe finds that cable at the closet end. A Wi-Fi analyzer only examines wireless signals.","Objective 2.8",["A Wi-Fi analyzer measures wireless signals and channels and cannot trace a copper cable.","A multimeter measures voltage, resistance and continuity, but it cannot pick one unlabeled cable out of a bundle at the far end.","A cable stripper removes the jacket from cable for termination; it does not identify cables.",null],2],["ap33",0,2,"A small business wants all web traffic from staff PCs to pass through a server that caches pages and filters blocked sites. Which device fits?",["A load balancer","A DNS server","A syslog server","A proxy server"],3,"A proxy makes requests on clients' behalf and can cache content and filter URLs. A load balancer spreads incoming traffic across several servers.","Objective 2.4",["A load balancer spreads incoming requests across several servers; it does not cache or filter staff web browsing.","A DNS server resolves names to addresses; DNS filtering can block some domains but it does not cache web pages for clients.","A syslog server only collects log messages from devices and does not handle web traffic.",null],2],["ap34",0,3,"A photo editor wants the most accurate colors and wide viewing angles on a budget LCD monitor. Which panel type should be chosen?",["TN","VA","IPS","CCFL-backlit TN"],2,"IPS panels give the best color accuracy and viewing angles among LCD types. TN panels are fast but shift color at an angle.","Objective 3.1",["TN panels are fast and cheap but have the weakest color accuracy and colors shift noticeably at an angle.","VA panels offer high contrast and deep blacks, but their color accuracy and viewing angles generally trail IPS.",null,"CCFL is an old backlight type and TN still has poor color and angles, so this is worse than a modern IPS panel."],1],["ap35",0,3,"A gamer notices motion blur and wants smoother animation. Which monitor specification matters most?",["A higher contrast ratio","A wider color gamut","A higher refresh rate","A larger screen size"],2,"A higher refresh rate (144 Hz or more) draws more frames per second, which smooths motion. Contrast ratio affects blacks and whites, not smoothness.","Objective 3.1",["Contrast ratio sets the difference between the darkest and brightest output; it does not reduce motion blur.","A wider color gamut shows more colors but does nothing for how smoothly motion is drawn.",null,"A larger screen changes size, not how many frames per second are shown, so it does not smooth animation."],2],["ap36",0,3,"A technician is installing a new SATA SSD in a desktop. Which two cables are needed?",["A Molex cable and an eSATA cable","A SATA data cable and a SATA power cable","An M.2 ribbon and a 4-pin fan cable","A USB header cable and a 24-pin cable"],1,"SATA drives take a 7-pin data cable from the motherboard and a 15-pin SATA power connector from the PSU. Molex is an older 4-pin power connector.","Objective 3.2",["Molex is an older 4-pin power connector, and eSATA is for external drives; neither is the standard pair for an internal SATA SSD.",null,"M.2 drives plug directly into a slot with no ribbon, and a 4-pin fan cable powers fans, not drives.","USB header cables serve front-panel ports, and the 24-pin cable powers the motherboard, not the SSD."],1],["ap37",0,3,"Cable must run through the drop-ceiling air return space of an office building. Which cable jacket is required?",["Plenum-rated","PVC riser-rated","Direct-burial","Outdoor UV-rated"],0,"Air-handling spaces need plenum-rated jackets, which give off less toxic smoke when burning. Riser-rated cable is for vertical runs between floors.","Objective 3.2",[null,"Riser-rated cable is designed for vertical runs between floors, not air-handling spaces, and does not meet plenum smoke requirements.","Direct-burial cable is built to go underground without conduit; its jacket is not rated for plenum air spaces.","UV-rated outdoor cable resists sunlight damage but does not meet the low-smoke requirement for plenum spaces."],1],["ap38",0,3,"What is the maximum recommended length of a Cat 6 run carrying Ethernet between a switch and a device?",["100 meters","50 meters","185 meters","500 meters"],0,"Twisted-pair Ethernet segments are limited to 100 meters. 185 m and 500 m were limits for old coax Ethernet.","Objective 3.2",[null,"50 meters is well within the limit; twisted-pair Ethernet segments can run up to 100 meters.","185 meters was the segment limit for thin coax Ethernet (10BASE2), not twisted-pair Cat 6.","500 meters was the segment limit for thick coax Ethernet (10BASE5), far beyond what Cat 6 supports."],1],["ap39",0,3,"A user wants to connect a monitor using a cable that carries both video and audio and is common on TVs. Which connector fits?",["VGA","DVI-D","HDMI","PS/2"],2,"HDMI carries digital video and audio and is standard on TVs and monitors. DVI-D carries digital video only, and VGA is analog video only.","Objective 3.2",["VGA carries analog video only, with no audio, and is rarely found on modern TVs.","DVI-D carries digital video but normally no audio, and it is uncommon on TVs.",null,"PS/2 is a legacy connector for keyboards and mice and carries no video or audio."],1],["ap40",0,3,"A technician tries to install a DDR4 module into a DDR5 motherboard slot, and it will not seat. Why?",["DDR5 slots need a BIOS update first","The module must be installed at an angle","The CPU is not seated correctly","The notch is in a different place"],3,"Each DDR generation has its notch in a different place so incompatible modules cannot be inserted. The generations are not electrically compatible either.","Objective 3.3",["A BIOS update cannot change the physical slot; DDR4 modules simply do not fit or work in DDR5 slots.","Desktop DIMMs go straight down into the slot; only laptop SODIMMs are inserted at an angle, and angle does not fix a mismatched notch.","CPU seating has no effect on whether a memory module physically fits into its slot.",null],2],["ap41",0,3,"A desktop has four memory slots and two matching modules. Following the motherboard manual, where should they go for best performance?",["Both side by side in slots 1 and 2","In any slots, since placement has no effect","In slots 3 and 4, leaving 1 and 2 empty for cooling","In matching-colored slots for dual channel"],3,"Installing matched modules in the paired slots (often color-coded) enables dual-channel mode, which doubles memory bandwidth.","Objective 3.3",["Adjacent slots are often on the same channel, so placing both modules side by side can leave the system in single-channel mode.","Placement matters: modules in the wrong slots may run in single-channel mode with half the memory bandwidth.","Leaving slots empty is not for cooling; the manual specifies the paired slots that enable dual channel, which may or may not be 3 and 4.",null],3],["ap42",0,3,"A server needs memory that can detect and correct single-bit errors. Which type should be ordered?",["SODIMM","ECC","Non-parity DDR4","Virtual RAM"],1,"Error-correcting code memory detects and fixes single-bit errors, which matters for servers. SODIMM describes a laptop form factor, not error correction.","Objective 3.3",["SODIMM is a small form factor used in laptops; it says nothing about error correction.",null,"Non-parity memory has no extra bits to detect or correct errors, which is the opposite of what the server needs.","Virtual RAM is disk space used as a page file extension; it is not a physical memory type and does not correct errors."],1],["ap43",0,3,"A small office wants a RAID array that survives a single drive failure and uses capacity efficiently. It has four drives. Which RAID level fits best?",["RAID 5","RAID 0","RAID 1","Spanned volume"],0,"RAID 5 stripes data with parity across three or more drives and survives one failure while losing only one drive's worth of capacity. RAID 0 has no redundancy.","Objective 3.4",[null,"RAID 0 stripes data with no redundancy, so a single drive failure loses the entire array.","RAID 1 mirrors drives and survives a failure, but with four drives it wastes half the capacity, making it less efficient than RAID 5.","A spanned volume just joins disks end to end with no redundancy, so one drive failure loses data."],3],["ap44",0,3,"What is the minimum number of drives needed for RAID 10?",["Two","Three","Four","Five"],2,"RAID 10 is a stripe of mirrors, so it needs at least two mirrored pairs, or four drives. RAID 1 needs two and RAID 5 needs three.","Objective 3.4",["Two drives is the minimum for RAID 1 (mirroring) or RAID 0, but RAID 10 needs at least two mirrored pairs.","Three drives is the minimum for RAID 5; RAID 10 cannot be built from an odd number of drives.",null,"Five drives is more than required, and RAID 10 uses an even number of drives since it is built from mirrored pairs."],1],["ap45",0,3,"A user wants to run Windows 11, which requires a TPM and Secure Boot. Where should the technician check that both are enabled?",["In Device Manager under Disk drives","In the router's admin page","In the UEFI firmware setup","In the PSU's voltage switch"],2,"TPM (often called PTT or fTPM) and Secure Boot are turned on in UEFI firmware settings. Device Manager cannot enable firmware features.","Objective 3.5",["Device Manager's Disk drives category lists storage devices; it cannot show or enable TPM and Secure Boot firmware settings.","The router's admin page configures the network and has no access to the PC's firmware security features.",null,"A PSU voltage switch selects AC input voltage and has nothing to do with TPM or Secure Boot."],2],["ap46",0,3,"A technician builds a small living-room PC and must choose the smallest common motherboard form factor. Which one fits?",["ATX","Mini-ITX","microATX","Extended ATX"],1,"Mini-ITX boards are 17 x 17 cm, smaller than microATX and ATX. Extended ATX is the largest and suits workstations.","Objective 3.5",["ATX is the standard full-size desktop board at about 30.5 x 24.4 cm, much larger than Mini-ITX.",null,"microATX is smaller than ATX but still larger than Mini-ITX, so it is not the smallest common option.","Extended ATX is the largest common form factor and is meant for workstations and servers, not small living-room builds."],1],["ap47",0,3,"After installing a new CPU cooler, a desktop shuts down within minutes under load. The technician finds the old thermal paste was cleaned off. What was most likely missed?",["Applying new thermal paste","Updating the chipset driver","Setting the RAM to dual channel","Enabling the TPM in firmware"],0,"Thermal paste fills tiny gaps between the CPU and heat sink; without it the CPU overheats and shuts down. Drivers do not affect heat transfer.","Objective 3.5",[null,"Chipset drivers manage motherboard features in the OS and have no effect on heat transfer from the CPU.","Dual-channel memory affects bandwidth, not CPU cooling, and would not cause thermal shutdowns.","The TPM stores cryptographic keys and is unrelated to CPU temperatures."],3],["ap48",0,3,"A user plans to add a high-end graphics card to a desktop. Which item must the technician check before buying?",["That the case has a front-panel USB-C port","That the network adapter supports Wi-Fi 6","That the monitor uses a VGA connector","That the PSU has enough wattage and power connectors"],3,"Powerful GPUs draw a lot of current and need PCIe power connectors, so the PSU must have the capacity and cables. Front-panel ports do not matter.","Objective 3.6",["A front-panel USB-C port is a convenience feature and has no bearing on whether a graphics card can be installed.","Wi-Fi 6 support in the network adapter is unrelated to installing or powering a graphics card.","Modern high-end cards rarely have VGA outputs, and the monitor connector is not the key pre-purchase check for power and fit.",null],3],["ap49",0,3,"A server has two power supplies connected to separate circuits. What is the main benefit?",["It doubles the available wattage for the CPU","It converts DC back to AC for the UPS","It keeps running if one supply fails","It lets the server use 3.3 V fans"],2,"Redundant PSUs let the system keep running when one supply or circuit fails. They share the load for resilience, not to double the power budget.","Objective 3.6",["Redundant supplies share the load for fault tolerance; the usable power budget does not double for the CPU.","PSUs convert AC to DC for components; they do not feed DC back to a UPS.",null,"Fan voltage is set by the supply's rails, not by having a second supply, and fans typically run on 12 V."],2],["ap50",0,3,"Which voltages does a standard ATX power supply provide to components?",["1.5 V, 9 V and 24 V","5 V, 48 V and 120 V","12 V, 110 V and 220 V","3.3 V, 5 V and 12 V"],3,"ATX supplies deliver 3.3, 5 and 12 V DC rails. 110/220 V is AC input from the wall, not output to components.","Objective 3.6",["These are not ATX rail voltages; 1.5 V is typical of DDR3 memory set by onboard regulators, and 9 V and 24 V are not ATX outputs.","48 V is typical of PoE and telecom equipment, and 120 V is AC wall power, not a DC output to components.","110 V and 220 V are AC input voltages from the wall; only 12 V here is an actual ATX output rail.",null],1],["ap51",0,3,"A laser printer prints a page. Which step comes right after the image is transferred to the paper?",["Exposing","Charging","Developing","Fusing"],3,"The order is processing, charging, exposing, developing, transferring, fusing and cleaning. Fusing melts toner onto the paper after transfer.","Objective 3.7",["Exposing happens before transfer, when the laser writes the image onto the charged drum.","Charging happens early, when the drum is given a uniform charge before exposure.","Developing, when toner is attracted to the exposed drum, happens before the image is transferred to paper.",null],1],["ap52",0,3,"A shipping desk prints receipts on heat-sensitive paper with no ink or toner. Which printer type is this?",["Impact","Inkjet","Color laser","Thermal"],3,"Thermal printers heat special paper to create the image. Impact printers strike an inked ribbon, which suits multipart forms.","Objective 3.7",["Impact printers strike an inked ribbon against the paper; they do use ink, so they don't match this description.","Inkjet printers spray liquid ink onto ordinary paper, so they are not ink-free.","Color laser printers fuse toner onto the page, which contradicts the no-toner description.",null],1],["ap53",0,3,"An office must print carbon-copy multipart invoices. Which printer type is required?",["Thermal","Inkjet","Impact","3-D"],2,"Only an impact (dot-matrix) printer physically strikes the page hard enough to mark all copies of a multipart form.","Objective 3.7",["Thermal printers heat special paper and cannot press through to mark the copies beneath the top sheet.","Inkjets spray ink on the top sheet only and cannot make carbon or carbonless copies.",null,"A 3-D printer builds physical objects from filament or resin and does not print on paper forms."],1],["ap54",0,3,"A laser printer shows a 'replace maintenance kit' message after 200,000 pages. Which parts does the kit usually contain?",["Fuser assembly and rollers","Toner cartridge and drum","Ink cartridges and printhead","Ribbon and platen"],0,"Laser maintenance kits usually contain a fuser, pickup and feed rollers and separation pads. Toner is replaced separately and more often.","Objective 3.8",[null,"The toner cartridge and drum are consumables replaced separately and much more often than a maintenance kit.","Ink cartridges and printheads belong to inkjet printers, not laser printers.","Ribbons and platens are parts of impact printers, not laser printers."],2],["ap55",0,3,"Users want a shared office printer to print on both sides by default to save paper. Where should the technician set this?",["The duplex setting in the printer's properties","The paper orientation in the application","The collate option in the print dialog","The printer's IP address settings"],0,"Setting duplex as the default in the printer's properties or on the device applies it to every job. Collation only orders multiple copies.","Objective 3.7",[null,"Orientation sets portrait or landscape layout and does not make the printer use both sides of the paper.","Collate controls the page order of multiple copies, not printing on both sides.","The IP address only determines how clients reach the printer; it has no effect on duplexing."],2],["ap56",0,4,"A technician installs a hypervisor directly on bare-metal server hardware with no host operating system. What type is it?",["Type 2","Type 1","Container runtime","Emulator"],1,"A Type 1 hypervisor runs directly on the hardware. A Type 2 hypervisor such as VirtualBox runs as an app on a host OS.","Objective 4.1",["A Type 2 hypervisor, such as VirtualBox, runs as an application on top of a host operating system.",null,"A container runtime runs isolated apps that share a host OS kernel, so it needs a host operating system.","An emulator imitates different hardware in software, usually on top of a host OS, rather than running directly on bare metal."],1],["ap57",0,4,"VirtualBox refuses to start a 64-bit virtual machine, saying hardware virtualization is unavailable. What should the technician check first?",["That the VM has a second virtual NIC","That VT-x or AMD-V is enabled in UEFI","That the host has a dedicated GPU","That the guest has Secure Boot disabled"],1,"64-bit guests need CPU virtualization extensions, which are often turned off in firmware. The number of virtual NICs has nothing to do with this error.","Objective 4.1",["Virtual NICs affect networking inside the VM; they do not cause a hardware virtualization error.",null,"A dedicated GPU is not required for hardware virtualization; the CPU extensions are what matter.","Secure Boot in the guest affects which boot loaders the VM trusts, not whether the host exposes VT-x or AMD-V."],3],["ap58",0,4,"An analyst wants to open a suspicious file without risking the company network. Which use of virtualization fits best?",["A VM bridged to the production LAN","A sandbox VM with no network","A container that shares the host's files","A cloud VM with a public IP address"],1,"An isolated sandbox VM contains any damage and can be reverted from a snapshot. Bridging it to the LAN would expose other systems.","Objective 4.1",["Bridging the VM to the production LAN gives any malware in the file a direct path to company systems.",null,"A container sharing host files lets malware reach the host's data, and containers also share the host kernel, so isolation is weaker.","A cloud VM with a public IP exposes the test system to the internet, and it is not isolated just because it is remote."],3],["ap59",0,4,"How do containers differ from virtual machines?",["They each include a full guest OS","They require a Type 1 hypervisor","They share the host's OS kernel","They cannot run on Linux"],2,"Containers package an app and its dependencies but share the host kernel, so they are lighter than VMs, which each run a full OS.","Objective 4.1",["Each VM includes a full guest OS; containers share the host kernel instead, which is why they are lighter.","Containers run on a normal host OS with a container engine and do not need a Type 1 hypervisor.",null,"Containers originated on Linux and run there natively, so this statement is the reverse of reality."],1],["ap60",0,4,"A company runs a 1990s accounting app that only works on an old OS. How can it keep using the app on new hardware?",["Run the old OS in a virtual machine","Install the app directly on Windows 11","Move the app to a SaaS email platform","Replace the PSU with a legacy model"],0,"Virtualization lets legacy operating systems and apps run on modern hardware in an isolated VM. Newer operating systems may not run the old app.","Objective 4.1",[null,"The app only works on the old OS, so installing it on Windows 11 is exactly what is likely to fail.","A SaaS email platform does not run custom accounting software; moving to it would not preserve the legacy app.","The PSU supplies power and has nothing to do with operating system or application compatibility."],2],["ap61",0,4,"A business rents virtual servers from a provider and manages the OS, patches and applications itself. Which cloud service model is this?",["SaaS","PaaS","IaaS","DaaS"],2,"With IaaS the provider supplies compute, storage and networking, and the customer manages everything from the OS up. With SaaS the customer only uses the app.","Objective 4.2",["With SaaS the provider runs the whole application stack and the customer only uses the software, managing no OS or patches.","With PaaS the provider manages the OS and runtime, and the customer only deploys code, not OS patches.",null,"Desktop as a Service delivers virtual desktops to users; it is not renting servers to manage the full OS and apps."],1],["ap62",0,4,"A retailer's cloud web servers add capacity automatically during holiday sales and scale back afterward. Which cloud characteristic is this?",["Multitenancy","Rapid elasticity","Shared responsibility","File synchronization"],1,"Rapid elasticity means resources scale out and in quickly with demand. Multitenancy means many customers share the provider's infrastructure.","Objective 4.2",["Multitenancy means many customers share the provider's infrastructure; it does not describe automatic scaling.",null,"Shared responsibility divides security duties between provider and customer; it is not about scaling capacity.","File synchronization keeps copies of files up to date across devices and has nothing to do with server capacity."],2],["ap63",0,4,"A company keeps sensitive databases in its own data center but runs its public website on a cloud provider, with the two connected. Which deployment model is this?",["Community cloud","Private cloud","Hybrid cloud","Public cloud"],2,"A hybrid cloud combines private and public clouds that work together. A community cloud is shared by organizations with common needs.","Objective 4.2",["A community cloud is shared by several organizations with common needs, not one company's own data center plus a public provider.","A private cloud is used by one organization alone and does not include the public cloud website.",null,"A purely public cloud would put everything, including the sensitive databases, on the provider's infrastructure."],1],["ap64",0,4,"A company pays its cloud provider only for the compute hours and storage it actually uses each month. Which characteristic does this describe?",["High availability","Metered utilization","Rapid elasticity","Shared responsibility"],1,"Metered (measured) utilization tracks consumption so customers pay as they go. Rapid elasticity is about scaling, not billing.","Objective 4.2",["High availability means keeping services running despite failures; it does not describe how usage is billed.",null,"Rapid elasticity describes scaling resources out and in with demand, not paying per use.","Shared responsibility splits security duties between provider and customer; it is not a billing model."],1],["ap65",0,4,"Employees log in to desktops that run on central servers and stream to thin clients. What is this called?",["NAS","SDN","KVM","VDI"],3,"Virtual desktop infrastructure hosts desktop VMs centrally and streams them to clients. A NAS provides file storage.","Objective 4.1",["A NAS is a file storage appliance on the network; it does not host or stream desktops.","Software-defined networking separates network control from forwarding hardware; it does not deliver desktops.","A KVM switch shares one keyboard, video and mouse among several local computers; it does not stream virtual desktops.",null],1],["ap66",0,5,"A technician has identified a problem and questioned the user. What is the next step in the CompTIA troubleshooting methodology?",["Implement the solution","Document findings and outcomes","Establish a theory of probable cause","Verify full system functionality"],2,"The order is identify, theorize, test the theory, plan, implement, verify, document. Implementing comes only after the theory is tested and a plan is made.","Objective 5.1",["Implementing a solution comes only after the theory is tested and a plan of action is made.","Documentation is the final step, after the fix is implemented and verified.",null,"Verifying full functionality happens after a solution is implemented, much later in the process."],1],["ap67",0,5,"Before replacing a failing drive in a user's PC, what should the technician do as part of identifying the problem?",["Back up the user's data if possible","Order a replacement motherboard","Document lessons learned","Escalate to the vendor"],0,"Identifying the problem includes making backups before changes and asking about recent changes. Documentation comes at the end of the process.","Objective 5.1",[null,"Ordering a motherboard is premature and wrong for a failing drive; parts are ordered after the cause is confirmed.","Documenting lessons learned belongs to the final step, not to identifying the problem.","Escalation happens when a theory cannot be confirmed or resolved, not as part of initial problem identification."],2],["ap68",0,5,"A technician's theory about a failing network card turns out to be wrong after testing. What should happen next?",["Implement the original fix anyway","Establish a new theory or escalate","Document the problem as resolved","Verify full system functionality"],1,"If the theory is not confirmed, re-establish a new theory or escalate. Applying an unproven fix wastes time and may cause new problems.","Objective 5.1",["Applying a fix based on a disproven theory wastes time and can introduce new problems without solving the real one.",null,"The problem is not resolved, so recording it as fixed would be inaccurate and leave the user without a solution.","Verification comes after a working solution is implemented; there is nothing to verify yet."],3],["ap69",0,5,"A desktop's date and time reset to a default each time it is unplugged. What is the most likely cause?",["A dead CMOS battery","A failing hard drive","An outdated graphics driver","A loose SATA cable"],0,"The CMOS/RTC battery keeps the clock and firmware settings alive without AC power. A weak one loses them every time power is removed.","Objective 5.2",[null,"A failing hard drive causes slow performance, errors or boot failures, but does not reset the firmware clock.","A graphics driver affects display output in the OS and has no role in keeping time when power is removed.","A loose SATA cable causes drive detection problems, not loss of the date and time settings."],2],["ap70",0,5,"A PC powers on and its fans spin, but there is no display and the motherboard beeps in a repeating pattern. What should the technician check first?",["The Windows Event Viewer logs","The monitor's color calibration","The keyboard layout settings","The POST code meaning in the board manual"],3,"Beep codes come from POST before the OS loads, and the manual maps each pattern to a fault such as memory or video. Windows logs do not exist yet.","Objective 5.2",["Beep codes occur during POST before Windows starts, so Event Viewer has no entries about this failure.","Color calibration adjusts how an already working display looks; it cannot explain no display plus beep codes.","Keyboard layout is an OS setting and has nothing to do with POST beep codes.",null],2],["ap71",0,5,"A workstation randomly shows blue screens with different stop codes, and a new memory module was installed last week. What should the technician run?",["A disk defragmenter","A DNS cache flush","A printer test page","A memory diagnostic"],3,"Random stop errors after a memory change suggest faulty or incompatible RAM; Windows Memory Diagnostic or MemTest86 will test it.","Objective 5.2",["Defragmenting reorganizes files on a hard drive and does not test for faulty memory.","Flushing the DNS cache clears stored name lookups and cannot cause or diagnose blue screens.","A printer test page only checks printing and is unrelated to system crashes.",null],2],["ap72",0,5,"A technician smells burning and finds swollen, leaking capacitors on a motherboard. What is the right action?",["Update the BIOS and retest","Reseat the RAM modules","Replace the motherboard","Apply new thermal paste"],2,"Bulging or leaking capacitors are a physical failure that firmware updates cannot fix; the board should be replaced.","Objective 5.2",["A BIOS update is software and cannot repair physically damaged capacitors; the board may also fail during flashing.","Reseating RAM addresses loose memory contacts, not swollen or leaking capacitors on the board.",null,"Thermal paste improves CPU cooling but does nothing for failed capacitors."],2],["ap73",0,5,"A PC shuts off after about 20 minutes of gaming, and the case feels very hot. Which cause should the technician check first?",["A wrong DNS server address","Clogged fans or dust buildup","A corrupted user profile","A full print queue"],1,"Heat-related shutdowns point to cooling problems such as dust, failed fans or dried thermal paste. DNS and profiles do not cause sudden power-offs.","Objective 5.2",["A wrong DNS server stops name resolution but cannot make a PC overheat and power off.",null,"A corrupted user profile causes login or settings problems, not heat-related shutdowns.","A full print queue delays printing and has no effect on system temperature or power."],2],["ap74",0,5,"A hard drive makes a loud clicking noise and the system has become very slow. What should the technician do?",["Defragment the drive to fix the noise","Increase the page file size","Disable S.M.A.R.T. alerts in firmware","Back up the data and replace the drive"],3,"Clicking often means a failing read/write head; save the data while possible and replace the drive. Defragmenting stresses a failing drive.","Objective 5.3",["Defragmenting cannot fix a mechanical fault and puts heavy read/write load on a drive that may be about to fail.","A larger page file does not address a failing drive and would put the paging file on the failing hardware.","Disabling S.M.A.R.T. alerts only hides the warnings and does nothing to protect the data.",null],2],["ap75",0,5,"A PC shows 'Bootable device not found' after a technician added a second drive. What is the most likely fix?",["Replace the power supply unit in the PC","Correct the boot order in UEFI","Update the video driver","Reset the router"],1,"Adding a drive can change the boot order so firmware tries the new, empty disk first. The PSU is working if the PC reaches this message.","Objective 5.3",["The PC powers on and displays a firmware message, so the power supply is working.",null,"The video driver loads with the OS; this error occurs before any OS or driver is loaded.","The router has nothing to do with local boot devices, so resetting it cannot fix this error."],3],["ap76",0,5,"A RAID 1 array reports a 'degraded' status in the management utility. What does this mean?",["One member drive has failed or dropped out","The array is rebuilding faster than normal","All data on the array is already lost","The array was converted to RAID 0"],0,"Degraded means the array is running without full redundancy; replace the failed drive and let it rebuild before a second drive fails.","Objective 5.3",[null,"Rebuilding happens after a failed drive is replaced; degraded means redundancy is currently missing, not that it is rebuilding fast.","Data on a degraded RAID 1 is still available from the remaining drive, though it is no longer protected.","RAID levels do not change on their own; degraded describes a missing or failed member, not a conversion."],2],["ap77",0,5,"CrystalDiskInfo shows a caution status with a rising reallocated sector count on a laptop drive. What does this indicate?",["The drive needs to be defragmented","The file system is FAT32 instead of NTFS","The drive's firmware is too new","The drive is failing and should be replaced soon"],3,"S.M.A.R.T. reallocated sectors mean the drive is remapping bad areas, a warning of coming failure. Back up and plan a replacement.","Objective 5.3",["Reallocated sectors are a physical health warning; defragmenting only rearranges files and puts extra stress on the drive.","The file system type does not affect S.M.A.R.T. attributes such as the reallocated sector count.","Newer firmware does not cause a rising reallocated sector count; that attribute reflects physical media wear.",null],3],["ap78",0,5,"A laptop display is very dim, but shining a flashlight on it shows the image faintly. Which component is most likely faulty?",["The digitizer","The backlight","The GPU driver","The Wi-Fi antenna"],1,"A faint image under a flashlight shows the LCD is drawing the picture but not being lit, which points to the backlight or its power.","Objective 5.4",["The digitizer handles touch input and does not light the display.",null,"A GPU driver problem would distort or fail to draw the image, but here the image is present and simply unlit.","Wi-Fi antennas run through the display lid but play no role in lighting the screen."],2],["ap79",0,5,"A new monitor shows text that looks fuzzy, and the image seems stretched. What should the technician check first?",["That the monitor has dead pixels","That the refresh rate is set to its minimum","That the resolution matches the native resolution","That the cable is plenum-rated"],2,"LCDs look sharp only at their native resolution; other settings are scaled and blurry. Dead pixels appear as fixed dots, not blur.","Objective 5.4",["Dead pixels show as fixed black or colored dots, not fuzzy text and a stretched image.","Lowering the refresh rate does not fix scaling and could cause flicker or other issues; the problem is resolution.",null,"Plenum rating concerns fire safety in air spaces and has no effect on image sharpness."],3],["ap80",0,5,"A projector keeps shutting down after 30 minutes in a conference room. Its lamp is new. What should the technician check?",["The HDMI cable for bent pins","The laptop's screen resolution","The projector's keystone setting","The air filter and vents for blockage"],3,"Projectors overheat and shut down when filters or vents are clogged. Keystone only corrects image shape.","Objective 5.4",["Bent HDMI pins would cause missing or glitchy video, not a shutdown after a set time.","The laptop's resolution affects the image but does not make the projector turn itself off after heating up.","Keystone correction only fixes a trapezoid-shaped image and cannot cause shutdowns.",null],3],["ap81",0,5,"A phone takes hours to charge and only charges when the cable is held at an angle. What should the technician check first?",["The phone's screen brightness","The Wi-Fi calling setting","The phone's storage space","The charging port for lint"],3,"Lint packed into the port stops the plug from seating fully. Cleaning it carefully often fixes charging without replacing the port.","Objective 5.5",["Brightness affects how fast the battery drains, but it cannot make charging depend on the cable's angle.","Wi-Fi calling only routes calls over Wi-Fi and has no effect on the physical charging connection.","Full storage slows the phone but does not stop the charging plug from making contact.",null],2],["ap82",0,5,"A smartphone has become hot to the touch and its battery drains quickly, even while idle. What should the technician check first?",["Apps using high background CPU","The installed case and screen protector","The ringtone volume level","The SIM card's PIN"],0,"Runaway apps keep the processor busy, which drains the battery and makes heat. Check battery usage by app and close or update the culprit.","Objective 5.5",[null,"A case can trap some heat, but it cannot explain a battery draining fast while the phone sits idle.","Ringtone volume uses power only when the phone rings and does not cause constant heat or idle drain.","A SIM PIN only protects the SIM card and has no effect on heat or battery usage."],3],["ap83",0,5,"A laptop's mouse pointer drifts across the screen on its own when nobody is touching it. What is a likely cause?",["A failed CMOS battery","Contamination on the touchpad","An incorrect DNS server","A wrong display refresh rate"],1,"Moisture, dirt or a swelling battery pressing on the touchpad can cause cursor drift. Clean the pad, check touchpad settings and inspect the battery.","Objective 5.5",["A dead CMOS battery resets the clock and firmware settings; it does not move the pointer.",null,"DNS settings affect name resolution only and cannot move the mouse pointer.","The refresh rate controls how often the screen redraws and would not make the pointer drift."],2],["ap84",0,5,"Laser-printed pages come out with toner that smears or rubs off easily. Which component is most likely failing?",["The pickup roller","The transfer belt's cleaning blade","The fuser","The network interface"],2,"The fuser uses heat and pressure to bond toner; if it fails, toner is not melted onto the page and smears. Pickup rollers only feed paper.","Objective 5.6",["A worn pickup roller causes misfeeds or jams, not toner that fails to bond to the paper.","The cleaning blade removes leftover toner; a failing one causes streaks or ghosting rather than unfused toner that rubs off.",null,"The network interface only receives print jobs and has no effect on how toner sticks to paper."],2],["ap85",0,5,"Every page from a laser printer shows a faint copy of an earlier image lower on the page. What is this called and what is the usual cause?",["Garbled print from a wrong driver","Ghosting from a worn drum or cleaning","Paper jams from worn feed rollers","Faded print from low toner"],1,"Ghost images come from the drum not being cleaned or discharged properly, often a worn drum or cleaning blade. Garbled text points to drivers.","Objective 5.6",["A wrong driver causes garbled symbols, not a faint repeat of an earlier image on the page.",null,"Worn feed rollers cause jams and misfeeds, not repeated ghost images.","Low toner makes the whole print faint, not a second copy of an earlier image lower on the page."],3],["ap86",0,5,"Users' print jobs stay stuck in the Windows print queue and new jobs pile up behind them. What should the technician try first?",["Replace the printer's fuser","Clear the queue and restart the spooler","Install a new toner cartridge","Change the paper tray settings"],1,"Stopping the Print Spooler service, clearing the stuck jobs and restarting it often releases a jammed queue. Hardware parts are not the cause.","Objective 5.6",["The fuser affects how toner bonds to paper; it cannot cause jobs to stick in the Windows print queue.",null,"Low toner produces faded pages but does not stop jobs from leaving the queue.","Tray settings may cause paper-size prompts but would not freeze the Windows spooler and pile up jobs."],2],["ap87",0,5,"A printer prints pages full of random symbols after being replaced with a different model. What is the most likely cause?",["The toner is low","The fuser is overheating","The old model's driver is still in use","The printer's pickup rollers are dirty"],2,"Garbled output usually comes from a wrong or corrupt driver that sends the wrong page description language. Low toner causes faded print.","Objective 5.6",["Low toner produces faded or light print, not random symbols.","An overheating fuser causes smearing or error codes, not pages of garbled characters.",null,"Dirty pickup rollers cause misfeeds and jams, not garbled content on the page."],3],["ap88",0,5,"A laptop reports 'No internet' and ipconfig shows 169.254.33.10. What does this address indicate?",["The PC has a valid public address","DNS resolution is working normally","The gateway is a Linux server","The PC could not get a DHCP lease"],3,"169.254.x.x is an APIPA address, assigned when no DHCP server responds. Check the cable or Wi-Fi link, the DHCP server and the scope.","Objective 5.7",["169.254.x.x is a link-local APIPA range and is never a valid public internet address.","The address shows the PC never got DHCP configuration, so it likely has no DNS server settings at all.","An APIPA address says nothing about the gateway's operating system; the PC never received a gateway from DHCP.",null],2],["ap89",0,5,"Wi-Fi in an office kitchen drops every time the microwave runs. What is the best fix?",["Lower the router's DHCP lease time","Change the DNS server to a public one","Switch the SSID to hidden","Move clients to the 5 GHz band"],3,"Microwave ovens radiate around 2.4 GHz and interfere with that band. The 5 GHz band avoids this interference.","Objective 5.7",["The lease time controls how long an IP address is assigned and has no effect on radio interference.","Changing DNS servers affects name resolution, not the 2.4 GHz interference that drops the wireless link.","Hiding the SSID only stops it from being broadcast; the network still uses the same interfered-with frequency.",null],3],["ap90",0,5,"A user's wired connection works but is much slower than expected, and the NIC shows it linked at 100 Mbps on a gigabit switch. What should the technician check first?",["The user's browser extensions","The DNS suffix search list","The patch cable for a damaged pair","The printer's IP address"],2,"Gigabit Ethernet needs all four pairs; a damaged pair often forces the link down to 100 Mbps. Test or replace the patch cable.","Objective 5.7",["Browser extensions can slow web pages but cannot force the NIC to negotiate a 100 Mbps link.","The DNS suffix search list affects how short names resolve, not the physical link speed.",null,"The printer's IP address has nothing to do with the user's NIC link speed."],3],["x1",0,1,"A finance employee edits spreadsheets on a laptop during train commutes and worries that nearby passengers can read the screen. Which accessory addresses this best?",["A privacy screen filter","A matte anti-glare protector","A laptop cable lock","A brighter replacement display"],0,"A privacy filter narrows the viewing angle so only the person directly in front can read the screen. An anti-glare protector reduces reflections but leaves the screen readable from the side.","Domain 1: Physical privacy",[null,"Anti-glare coatings cut reflections but the screen stays readable from the side.","A cable lock stops theft of the laptop, not people reading the screen.","A brighter display makes the screen easier for neighbors to read, not harder."],1],["x2",0,1,"A traveler wants to add a local carrier's data plan while abroad without opening the SIM tray. The phone supports it. What should the user do?",["Enable airplane mode and rejoin Wi-Fi","Install an eSIM profile from the carrier","Tap the phone on an NFC kiosk","Pair with a local friend's phone over Bluetooth tethering"],1,"An eSIM is a built-in SIM that can be loaded with a carrier profile, often from a QR code, with no physical card. Airplane mode turns radios off and does not add a carrier plan.","Domain 1: Cellular connectivity",["Airplane mode turns radios off; it does not add a carrier plan.",null,"NFC is for short-range payments and pairing, not for adding carrier plans.","Bluetooth pairing does not give the phone its own local carrier plan."],2],["x3",0,1,"On a flight, a passenger must turn off the phone's cellular radio but still wants the in-flight Wi-Fi and Bluetooth earbuds. What should they do?",["Power the phone off for the flight","Turn off location services only","Use airplane mode, then turn Wi-Fi and Bluetooth back on","Take the SIM card out of the phone"],2,"Airplane mode disables all radios, and Wi-Fi and Bluetooth can then be switched back on while cellular stays off. Turning off location services leaves the cellular radio running.","Domain 1: Cellular connectivity",["Powering off also removes Wi-Fi and Bluetooth, which the user wants to keep.","Location services are separate; the cellular radio would stay on.",null,"Pulling the SIM does not turn off every cellular radio function and is clumsy."],2],["x4",0,1,"Employees use personal phones for company email but refuse to let IT control the whole device. The company must be able to wipe only corporate data if a phone is lost. What fits best?",["A full device wipe through MDM","A factory reset policy for lost phones","A screen lock rule for every personal app","App management (MAM) of company apps"],3,"Mobile application management controls and can wipe only the managed company apps and their data, leaving personal content alone. A full MDM wipe would erase the employee's personal data too.","Domain 1: MDM and MAM",["A full MDM wipe erases the employee's personal data as well.","A factory reset also wipes personal data, which employees refuse.","A screen lock protects access but cannot remove company data remotely.",null],3],["x5",0,1,"After a laptop's display assembly is replaced, the webcam and built-in microphone no longer work, though the picture is fine. What was most likely missed?",["The webcam/mic cable in the display bezel","The Wi-Fi card's driver update","The keyboard's ribbon cable","The CMOS battery connector"],0,"The webcam and often the microphones sit in the display bezel and use their own cable, which must be reconnected. The Wi-Fi card driver has nothing to do with the camera, even though antennas also run through the lid.","Domain 1: Screen parts",[null,"The Wi-Fi driver has nothing to do with the camera or microphone.","The keyboard cable is in the base and does not serve the display bezel.","The CMOS battery keeps firmware settings and time, not camera function."],2],["x6",0,1,"A user wiped an old phone after moving to a new one and now cannot get codes from the company's authenticator app. What should the user have done before wiping the old phone?",["Backed up photos to cloud storage","Moved or re-enrolled the authenticator accounts","Turned off Bluetooth and Wi-Fi on the old phone first","Copied the contacts stored on the SIM"],1,"Authenticator accounts must be transferred or re-enrolled on the new device before the old one is wiped. A photo backup does not move the secrets that generate the codes.","Domain 1: Authenticator apps",["Photo backups do not include the secrets that generate the codes.",null,"Bluetooth settings have no part in authenticator codes.","SIM contacts do not hold authenticator accounts."],2],["x7",0,1,"A laptop has a built-in fingerprint sensor. Users want to sign in quickly without typing a password where others can see. Which option fits?",["A pattern unlock on the lock screen","A privacy filter over the screen","Fingerprint biometric sign-in","A one-minute screen timeout"],2,"Enrolling a fingerprint lets users sign in with the biometric sensor instead of typing a password. A privacy filter hides the screen but does not change how the user signs in.","Domain 1: Biometrics",["A pattern is still something others can watch being entered.","A privacy filter hides the screen but does not change how users sign in.",null,"A screen timeout locks the laptop sooner but still needs a password to sign in."],1],["x8",0,1,"An older laptop has a 2.5-inch SATA drive bay and no M.2 slot. The user wants much faster boot times. What is the best upgrade?",["An M.2 NVMe SSD with an adapter","A 3.5-inch 7200 RPM hard drive","A USB flash drive as the boot device","A 2.5-inch SATA SSD"],3,"A 2.5-inch SATA SSD fits the existing bay and is far faster than a hard drive. An M.2 NVMe drive needs an M.2 slot with PCIe, which this laptop lacks.","Domain 1: Laptop storage",["The laptop has no M.2 slot, so an NVMe drive cannot be used.","A 3.5-inch desktop drive will not fit a laptop bay and is slower than an SSD.","Booting from USB flash is slow and unreliable for a daily system drive.",null],2],["x9",0,2,"A firewall sits between client PCs and the DHCP server. Which traffic must be allowed for clients to get addresses?",["UDP 67 and 68","TCP 67 and 68","UDP 161 and 162","TCP 20 and 21"],0,"DHCP uses UDP, with the server on port 67 and the client on port 68. TCP 67 and 68 is wrong because DHCP never uses TCP.","Domain 2: Ports and protocols",[null,"DHCP runs over UDP, not TCP, on these ports.","UDP 161 and 162 are SNMP polling and traps, not DHCP.","TCP 20 and 21 are FTP data and control, not DHCP."],1],["x10",0,2,"A monitoring system polls switches for interface counters and receives alerts the switches send when a link goes down. Which protocol does this?",["LDAP on TCP 389","SNMP on UDP 161 and 162","Telnet on TCP 23","SMB on TCP 445"],1,"SNMP polls devices on UDP 161 and receives traps on UDP 162. LDAP queries directory services such as Active Directory and does not collect device statistics.","Domain 2: Ports and protocols",["LDAP is for directory lookups such as user accounts, not device counters.",null,"Telnet is a remote command line and does not collect statistics or traps.","SMB is Windows file sharing and has no role in device monitoring."],1],["x11",0,2,"An old switch is managed with a protocol that sends passwords in plain text on port 23. What should replace it for secure command-line management?",["RDP on port 3389","HTTP on port 80","SSH on port 22","FTP on port 21"],2,"SSH on port 22 gives an encrypted command-line session and replaces Telnet on port 23. RDP gives a graphical Windows desktop, not a switch command line.","Domain 2: Ports and protocols",["RDP provides a Windows graphical desktop, not switch command-line access.","HTTP is unencrypted web traffic, so passwords would still be exposed.",null,"FTP transfers files and also sends passwords in plain text."],1],["x12",0,2,"A user reads email on a phone and a laptop and wants read status and folders to match on both, with mail kept on the server. Which protocol should the mail client use?",["POP3 on port 110","SMTP on port 25","LDAP on port 389","IMAP on port 143"],3,"IMAP keeps mail and folders on the server, so every device sees the same state. POP3 usually downloads mail to one device, so the phone and laptop drift apart.","Domain 2: Ports and protocols",["POP3 usually downloads mail to one device, so the two devices drift apart.","SMTP sends mail between servers; it does not sync mailboxes to clients.","LDAP is a directory protocol, not a way to read email.",null],2],["x13",0,2,"Which DNS record type maps a host name to an IPv6 address?",["AAAA","A","MX","CNAME"],0,"An AAAA record maps a name to a 128-bit IPv6 address. An A record does the same job for IPv4 addresses only.","Domain 2: DNS records",[null,"An A record maps a name to an IPv4 address, not IPv6.","An MX record names the mail servers for a domain.","A CNAME points one name at another name, not at an address."],1],["x14",0,2,"A company already publishes SPF and signs mail with DKIM. It now wants receiving servers told what to do with mail that fails these checks, and to send back reports. Which record should it add?",["An MX record for a backup server","A DMARC policy in a TXT record","A CNAME pointing to the mail host","An AAAA record for the mail server"],1,"DMARC, published as a TXT record, tells receivers how to treat mail that fails SPF and DKIM alignment and where to send reports. An MX record only says which servers accept the domain's mail.","Domain 2: DNS records",["MX records only say which servers accept mail for the domain.",null,"A CNAME is an alias and cannot carry a mail handling policy.","An AAAA record gives an IPv6 address and says nothing about failed mail."],2],["x15",0,2,"A home gets fiber-to-the-premises service. Which device at the house converts the optical signal for the customer's router?",["A cable modem","A DSL modem","An optical network terminal (ONT)","A PoE injector"],2,"An ONT terminates the fiber and hands off Ethernet to the router. A cable modem works on coaxial cable service, not on fiber.","Domain 2: Networking hardware",["A cable modem works with coaxial cable service, not fiber.","A DSL modem works over telephone lines, not fiber.",null,"A PoE injector adds power to Ethernet; it does not terminate fiber."],1],["x16",0,2,"An office buys Wi-Fi 6E access points to get away from crowded channels. Which band does 6E add?",["2.4 GHz","900 MHz","60 GHz","6 GHz"],3,"Wi-Fi 6E extends 802.11ax into the 6 GHz band, which has many clean channels. 2.4 GHz is the older, crowded band that the office wants to avoid.","Domain 2: Wireless",["2.4 GHz is the older, crowded band the office wants to avoid.","900 MHz is not a Wi-Fi 6E band.","60 GHz is used by a different short-range standard, not Wi-Fi 6E.",null],1],["x17",0,2,"A security review finds that a game console opened inbound ports on a SOHO router by itself. Which router feature allowed this, and why is it a risk?",["UPnP, since any device can open ports with no admin approval","DMZ, since it hides every device behind the router's NAT","WPA3, since it lets clients change the router settings","DHCP reservation, since it opens a port for each MAC"],0,"UPnP lets devices, and any malware on them, open port forwards automatically without the admin knowing. A DMZ setting exposes one host deliberately; it does not let devices open their own ports.","Domain 2: SOHO setup",[null,"A DMZ exposes one chosen host; it does not let devices open their own ports.","WPA3 is Wi-Fi encryption and does not let clients change router settings.","A DHCP reservation fixes an IP address and does not open any ports."],3],["x18",0,2,"What happens when a SOHO router's DMZ setting is pointed at one internal PC?",["All inbound traffic to that PC is blocked","Unsolicited inbound traffic is sent to that PC","A separate guest Wi-Fi network is created for visitors","Each device receives its own public IP"],1,"A SOHO DMZ host receives all unsolicited inbound traffic that does not match another rule, which exposes that PC. It does not block traffic; it is the opposite of protection.","Domain 2: SOHO setup",["A DMZ host is exposed to inbound traffic, the opposite of blocking it.",null,"Guest Wi-Fi is a separate feature and is not created by the DMZ setting.","The router still has one public IP; devices do not each get one."],2],["x19",0,2,"ipconfig shows a PC's IPv6 address beginning with fe80::. What type of address is this?",["A global unicast address","A multicast address","A link-local address","The loopback address"],2,"fe80:: addresses are link-local; every IPv6 interface has one and they are not routed beyond the local link. The IPv6 loopback address is ::1, not an fe80:: address.","Domain 2: IP addressing",["Global unicast addresses are routable and usually begin with 2 or 3, not fe80.","IPv6 multicast addresses begin with ff, not fe80.",null,"The IPv6 loopback address is ::1."],1],["x20",0,2,"An online store runs three identical web servers and wants requests spread across them, with traffic moved away from any server that fails. Which device fits?",["A proxy server","A spam gateway","A syslog server","A load balancer"],3,"A load balancer shares client requests among servers and stops sending to one that fails its health checks. A proxy server acts for clients going out, caching and filtering, rather than spreading load across servers.","Domain 2: Networked host services",["A proxy acts for clients going out; it does not spread load across servers.","A spam gateway filters email, not web requests.","A syslog server collects logs and does not handle client requests.",null],2],["x21",0,2,"The network admin wants routers, firewalls and servers to send their log messages to one central place for review. What should be set up?",["A syslog server","An AAA server","A DHCP server","A file server"],0,"A syslog server collects log messages sent by many devices in one place. An AAA server handles authentication and accounting, not general device logs.","Domain 2: Networked host services",[null,"An AAA server handles authentication and accounting, not device logs.","A DHCP server hands out addresses and does not collect logs.","A file server stores shared files and is not built to receive log messages."],1],["x22",0,2,"A technician suspects a PC's network port is faulty and wants to test it with no switch or network attached. Which tool helps?",["A toner and probe","A loopback plug","A Wi-Fi analyzer","A punchdown tool"],1,"A loopback plug sends the port's transmit signal back to its receive pins so the port can test itself. A toner and probe traces cable runs but does not test a network port.","Domain 2: Networking tools",["A toner and probe traces cable runs; it does not test a port.",null,"A Wi-Fi analyzer checks wireless signals, not a wired port.","A punchdown tool terminates wires on a block; it does not test anything."],2],["x23",0,3,"A technician terminates one end of a patch cable as T568A and the other end as T568B. What kind of cable results?",["A straight-through cable","A rollover cable","A crossover cable","A plenum cable"],2,"Wiring one end A and the other end B swaps the transmit and receive pairs, making a crossover cable. A straight-through cable uses the same standard on both ends.","Domain 3: Cables and connectors",["A straight-through cable uses the same standard on both ends.","A rollover cable reverses all pins and is used for console connections.",null,"Plenum describes the jacket's fire rating, not the pin wiring."],3],["x24",0,3,"A network link must run 400 meters between two buildings, through an area with heavy electrical interference. Which cable fits best?",["Cat 6 unshielded twisted pair","Cat 5e shielded twisted pair","RG-6 coaxial cable","Fiber optic cable"],3,"Fiber carries light, so it is immune to electrical interference and covers far longer distances than copper. Shielded Cat 5e helps with interference but is still limited to about 100 meters for Ethernet.","Domain 3: Cables and connectors",["Unshielded copper suffers from interference and is limited to about 100 m.","Shielding helps with noise, but copper Ethernet still stops at about 100 m.","Coax is not used for this kind of building-to-building Ethernet link.",null],2],["x25",0,3,"A user buys an M.2 NVMe SSD for a laptop whose M.2 slot supports only SATA drives. What will happen?",["The drive will not work, as the slot has no NVMe support","The drive runs at full NVMe speed in the slot","The drive falls back to SATA speed on its own","The drive will work once a new chipset driver is installed"],0,"An NVMe drive needs PCIe lanes, so a SATA-only M.2 slot cannot use it even if it fits. NVMe drives do not fall back to SATA mode; the connector is shared but the interface is not.","Domain 3: Storage",[null,"The slot has no PCIe lanes, so NVMe speed is not possible.","NVMe drives do not have a SATA fallback mode.","No driver can add PCIe lanes that the slot does not have."],3],["x26",0,3,"A six-drive array must keep running after ANY two drives fail at the same time. Which RAID level fits best?",["RAID 5","RAID 6","RAID 10","RAID 1 on two drives"],1,"RAID 6 stores two sets of parity, so any two drives can fail. RAID 10 survives two failures only if they are in different mirror pairs, so not any two drives.","Domain 3: RAID",["RAID 5 has one set of parity and survives only one drive failure.",null,"RAID 10 fails if both drives in one mirror pair fail.","A two-drive mirror does not use six drives and cannot lose both drives."],3],["x27",0,3,"A laptop's USB-C port has a small lightning bolt symbol next to it. What does this usually indicate?",["It is a charge-only port","It is a Lightning port","It supports Thunderbolt","It only outputs VGA video"],2,"A lightning bolt next to a USB-C port marks Thunderbolt support, which carries fast data, displays and docks. Lightning is Apple's own connector on older iPhones, not a USB-C port.","Domain 3: Cables and connectors",["On a USB-C port the lightning bolt marks Thunderbolt support, not a port limited to charging.","Lightning is Apple's own connector, not a USB-C port.",null,"USB-C ports do not output VGA; VGA needs an adapter."],1],["x28",0,3,"A company needs dedicated, tamper-resistant hardware to create and store many encryption keys used by a group of web servers. What should it buy?",["A TPM chip on each web server's system board","Secure Boot turned on in UEFI","A BIOS password on each server","A hardware security module (HSM)"],3,"An HSM is a dedicated device built to generate, store and use many keys securely for servers. A TPM is tied to a single machine and is meant to protect that one system.","Domain 3: Firmware and security",["A TPM protects one machine; it is not a shared key store for many servers.","Secure Boot checks boot software signatures; it does not store keys for servers.","A BIOS password blocks firmware changes and has nothing to do with key storage.",null],2],["x29",0,3,"Users keep changing the boot order to start lab PCs from their own USB drives. What stops this most directly?",["A firmware supervisor password","Enabling the TPM in firmware","Disabling Secure Boot","A Windows screen saver password"],0,"A UEFI/BIOS supervisor password stops users from changing firmware settings such as boot order. A Windows password does nothing once someone reaches the firmware setup screen.","Domain 3: Firmware",[null,"The TPM stores keys; it does not lock the boot order.","Turning off Secure Boot would make booting other media easier, not harder.","A Windows password does not protect firmware settings."],2],["x30",0,3,"A company is looking at thin laptops with ARM processors instead of x86. What is the main thing to check first?",["Whether ARM chips can run any operating system","Whether its x86 apps have ARM builds or run well emulated","Whether ARM laptops always need liquid cooling to run safely","Whether ARM processors are sold with integrated graphics"],1,"ARM uses a different instruction set, so x86-only software needs an ARM version or emulation, which may be slower or unsupported. ARM chips do run full operating systems, so that is not the concern.","Domain 3: CPUs",["ARM processors do run full operating systems, including Windows and Linux.",null,"ARM laptops are known for low power use and usually need little cooling.","Most ARM laptop chips do include integrated graphics."],3],["x31",0,3,"A CPU is listed as 8 cores and 16 threads. What provides the 16 threads?",["Two physical CPU packages","Its integrated graphics unit","Two threads per core (SMT)","Dual-channel memory"],2,"Simultaneous multithreading, such as Intel Hyper-Threading, lets each core run two threads at once. It is one CPU package, not two; eight cores times two threads gives 16.","Domain 3: CPUs",["It is one CPU package; eight cores are counted in that one chip.","Integrated graphics handle display output, not extra CPU threads.",null,"Memory channels affect bandwidth, not how many threads the CPU runs."],1],["x32",0,3,"A streamer wants to record gameplay from a game console's HDMI output on a desktop PC. Which expansion card is needed?",["A sound card","A network interface card","A graphics card with more VRAM","A video capture card"],3,"A capture card takes an HDMI video input and makes it available to recording software. A graphics card outputs video from the PC; it does not accept video from another device.","Domain 3: Expansion cards",["A sound card handles audio and cannot take in HDMI video.","A NIC connects to networks and does not accept video input.","A graphics card sends video out; it does not capture a console's output.",null],1],["x33",0,3,"A user moves a desktop from the US to Europe. The PSU label says 100-240 V auto-ranging input. What is needed?",["Only a power cord for the local outlet","A new PSU rated for 240 V","A step-up transformer","A switch set to 220 V output"],0,"An auto-ranging PSU accepts any input from 100 to 240 V, so only the right cord or plug is needed. A transformer is only needed for fixed-voltage devices, which this PSU is not.","Domain 3: Power supplies",[null,"A new PSU is not needed, since the current one already accepts 240 V.","Transformers are for fixed-voltage devices; this PSU is auto-ranging.","PSU outputs are low DC voltages; there is no 220 V output switch."],2],["x34",0,3,"A PC builder wants to cut cable clutter by attaching only the power cables the build actually needs. Which PSU type fits?",["A redundant PSU","A modular PSU","A higher-wattage PSU","A dual-rail PSU"],1,"Modular PSUs have detachable cables, so unused ones can be left out. A redundant PSU adds a second supply for uptime and does nothing for cable clutter.","Domain 3: Power supplies",["A redundant PSU adds a backup supply; it does not reduce cables.",null,"More wattage does not change how many cables are attached.","Rail design affects power delivery, not whether cables can be removed."],1],["x35",0,3,"Confidential documents are left sitting in a shared printer's output tray. Which feature holds each job until the user enters a PIN at the printer?",["Duplex printing","Collation","Secure print","Tray assignment"],2,"Secure print holds jobs until the user authenticates at the device, so pages are not left out. Collation only orders the pages in multi-copy jobs.","Domain 3: Printers",["Duplex prints on both sides and does not hold jobs.","Collation orders pages in multi-copy jobs; it does not hold them.",null,"Tray assignment picks the paper source, not when a job is released."],2],["x36",0,3,"Which consumable does a typical desktop 3-D printer that builds parts layer by layer from melted plastic use?",["Toner powder","An ink ribbon","Thermal paper","Plastic filament"],3,"Fused-filament 3-D printers melt plastic filament and lay it down in layers. Toner is the powder used by laser printers, not 3-D printers.","Domain 3: Printer types",["Toner is the powder used by laser printers.","Ink ribbons are used by impact printers.","Thermal paper is used by thermal receipt printers.",null],1],["x37",0,3,"An office wants a printer that every PC can use even when no particular user's PC is switched on. What is the best setup?",["Give it its own network connection and fixed IP","Share it from one user's desktop PC","Move its USB cable from PC to PC","Pair it with each PC over Bluetooth"],0,"A network printer with a fixed address or reservation works for everyone without depending on a host PC. Sharing from a desktop fails whenever that desktop is off.","Domain 3: Printers",[null,"Sharing from one desktop fails whenever that PC is turned off.","Moving the cable between PCs is impractical and only one PC can print at a time.","Bluetooth pairing is short-range and does not suit a shared office printer."],2],["x38",0,4,"A test VM must appear on the office LAN with its own address from the office DHCP server so other PCs can reach it. Which VM network mode fits?",["NAT","Bridged","Internal network","No network adapter"],1,"Bridged mode connects the VM straight to the physical network, so it gets a LAN address like any other PC. NAT hides the VM behind the host's address, so other PCs cannot easily reach it.","Domain 4: VM networking",["NAT hides the VM behind the host, so other PCs cannot easily reach it.",null,"An internal network isolates the VM from the LAN entirely.","Without a network adapter the VM has no network access at all."],2],["x39",0,4,"Two lab VMs used for malware training must talk to each other but must not reach the host's LAN or the internet. Which VM network setting fits?",["Bridged to the host adapter","NAT through the host","An internal network","Shared folders with the host"],2,"An internal network links VMs only to each other, with no path to the LAN or internet. NAT would still let the VMs reach outside networks through the host.","Domain 4: VM networking",["Bridged mode puts the VMs on the LAN, which must be avoided.","NAT still lets the VMs reach outside networks through the host.",null,"Shared folders connect VMs to host files, which does not isolate them."],2],["x40",0,4,"Before applying a risky update inside a test VM, a technician wants a quick way to return to the current state. What should they do?",["Clone the host's physical drive","Add more RAM to the VM","Turn on nested virtualization","Take a snapshot of the VM"],3,"A snapshot saves the VM's state so it can be rolled back quickly if the update breaks something. Cloning the host drive is slow and is not needed to roll back one VM.","Domain 4: VM security",["Cloning the host drive is slow and unnecessary to roll back one VM.","More RAM does not give any way to undo the update.","Nested virtualization runs hypervisors inside VMs and has nothing to do with rollback.",null],1],["x41",0,4,"A manager says the VMs on a server are safe because the host OS is fully patched. Is that correct?",["No, each guest OS must still be patched","Yes, VMs inherit the host's patches","Yes, the hypervisor blocks all guest malware","No, but only Type 2 guests need patches"],0,"Each guest has its own operating system and apps, which need their own updates. Guests do not inherit host patches; the host and guests are patched separately.","Domain 4: VM security",[null,"Guests run their own operating systems and do not inherit host patches.","Hypervisors isolate VMs but do not scan or block malware in guests.","Both Type 1 and Type 2 guests need their own patches."],2],["x42",0,4,"Developers want to deploy their web code without managing servers, operating systems or runtime patches. Which cloud service model fits?",["IaaS virtual machines","Platform as a service","A private data center","Desktop as a service"],1,"PaaS provides a managed platform where developers deploy code while the provider handles the OS and runtime. With IaaS the customer still patches and manages the operating system.","Domain 4: Cloud service models",["IaaS still leaves the OS and runtime patching to the customer.",null,"A private data center means the company manages all servers itself.","DaaS delivers desktops to users, not a platform for deploying code."],2],["x43",0,4,"Several hospitals share cloud infrastructure built for their common privacy and compliance needs, and no one else uses it. Which deployment model is this?",["Public cloud","Hybrid cloud","Community cloud","A private cloud for each"],2,"A community cloud is shared by organizations with common requirements, such as healthcare rules. A public cloud is open to any customer, not limited to one group.","Domain 4: Cloud deployment models",["A public cloud is open to any customer, not limited to one group.","Hybrid mixes private and public clouds; it does not describe shared use by a group.",null,"Separate private clouds would not be shared among the hospitals."],2],["x44",0,4,"A host with 16 GB of RAM runs a Type 2 hypervisor. Three VMs are each given 6 GB, and the last one fails to start or everything runs very slowly. What is the most likely cause?",["The guest operating systems are too new","NAT networking is slowing down each VM's memory access","Type 2 hypervisors cannot run three VMs","The VMs' RAM plus the host's needs exceed 16 GB"],3,"Three 6 GB VMs need 18 GB before the host OS takes its share, which is more than is installed. Type 2 hypervisors can run several VMs; the limit here is memory, not the hypervisor type.","Domain 4: VM resources",["Newer guest OS versions do not cause the host to run out of memory.","NAT networking affects traffic, not how much RAM the VMs use.","Type 2 hypervisors can run several VMs; memory is the limit here.",null],3],["x45",0,5,"After replacing a failed NIC, a technician confirms the user can reach shares and the internet, and considers steps to prevent a repeat. Which troubleshooting step is this?",["Verify full system functionality","Establish a plan of action","Test the theory to find the cause","Identify the problem"],0,"Verifying full functionality, and adding preventive measures if needed, comes after the fix is implemented. Establishing a plan of action happens before the fix, not after.","Domain 5: Troubleshooting methodology",[null,"Planning the fix happens before it is implemented, not after.","Testing the theory is done before the fix to confirm the cause.","Identifying the problem is the first step, not a check after the fix."],2],["x46",0,5,"A technician confirms that a busy file server needs a firmware update that requires a reboot. It is mid-morning on a workday. What should happen next?",["Apply the update and reboot right away","Plan the fix, checking impact and company policies","Document the findings and close the ticket","Question the users again about the symptoms they saw"],1,"After confirming the theory, the next step is a plan of action that weighs impact and follows change policies, such as scheduling the reboot. Rebooting at once would disrupt everyone using the server.","Domain 5: Troubleshooting methodology",["Rebooting a busy server mid-morning would disrupt everyone using it.",null,"Nothing has been fixed yet, so it is too early to document and close.","The cause is already confirmed; more questioning does not move things on."],3],["x47",0,5,"What is the final step of the CompTIA troubleshooting methodology?",["Verify full system functionality","Implement the solution","Document findings, actions and outcomes","Test the theory to determine the cause"],2,"Documenting findings, actions and outcomes is the last step, so others can learn from the fix. Verifying functionality comes just before it, not last.","Domain 5: Troubleshooting methodology",["Verifying functionality comes just before documentation, not last.","Implementing the solution is in the middle of the process.",null,"Testing the theory is an early step, well before the fix."],1],["x48",0,5,"Windows warns of a duplicate IP address after a user set a static address on their PC. The network uses DHCP. What is the best fix?",["Change the PC's DNS server","Replace the PC's network card","Shorten the DHCP lease time","Return the PC to DHCP"],3,"Switching the PC back to DHCP gives it a unique address from the scope, ending the conflict. Shortening the lease does not stop a manually set address from clashing.","Domain 5: Network problems",["DNS settings do not affect a duplicate IP address.","The NIC is fine; the problem is the address typed into it.","A shorter lease does not stop a manually set address from clashing.",null],2],["x49",0,5,"A new 5 GHz-only SSID appears on most laptops, but one older laptop cannot see it at all. What is the most likely cause?",["Its wireless adapter supports only 2.4 GHz","The DHCP scope has run out of addresses","The laptop has the wrong WPA3 passphrase saved","The office DNS server is not responding"],0,"An adapter that only supports 2.4 GHz cannot see networks on 5 GHz. A wrong passphrase stops the laptop joining, but the SSID would still appear in the list.","Domain 5: Network problems",[null,"A full DHCP scope stops address assignment but the SSID would still be visible.","A wrong passphrase stops joining, but the SSID would still be listed.","DNS problems do not stop a laptop from seeing a wireless network name."],3],["x50",0,5,"A PC can ping 8.8.8.8, but web browsing by name fails. Which tool should the technician use to test name resolution?",["tracert","nslookup","ipconfig /release","netstat"],1,"nslookup queries DNS servers directly, showing whether names resolve. tracert shows the path to a host, which already works here since ping by IP succeeds.","Domain 5: Diagnostic tools",["tracert shows the path to a host, which already works since ping by IP succeeds.",null,"Releasing the IP address would break the working connection, not test DNS.","netstat shows current connections and ports, not name resolution results."],1],["x51",0,5,"A user reports slow access to a remote office site. The technician wants to see at which hop along the path the delay begins. Which tool fits?",["ping","nslookup","tracert","ipconfig"],2,"tracert lists each router hop with response times, showing where delay starts. ping only measures the round trip to the final host, not each hop.","Domain 5: Diagnostic tools",["ping measures the round trip to the final host only, not each hop.","nslookup tests DNS resolution, not delay along the path.",null,"ipconfig shows local address settings, not path delay."],1],["x52",0,5,"A switch log shows one port going up and down repeatedly, and the user on that port keeps losing the connection. What should the technician check first?",["The DNS server settings on the PC","The browser's cache and cookies","The PC's printer drivers","The patch cable and NIC link"],3,"Port flapping usually comes from a bad cable, loose connector or failing NIC at the physical layer. DNS settings affect name lookup, not whether the link stays up.","Domain 5: Network problems",["DNS settings affect name lookup, not whether the link stays up.","Browser cache cannot make a switch port go up and down.","Printer drivers have nothing to do with the network link state.",null],2],["x53",0,5,"VoIP calls sound choppy. Ping shows a normal average latency, but individual response times vary widely. What is the issue and a fix?",["Jitter; apply QoS priority for voice","APIPA; renew the DHCP lease","DNS failure; flush the resolver cache","IP conflict; set a static address"],0,"Widely varying delay is jitter, which breaks up real-time voice; QoS gives voice traffic priority. DNS problems stop name lookups but do not make an established call choppy.","Domain 5: Network problems",[null,"APIPA would mean no DHCP address, and the call would not work at all.","DNS problems stop lookups but do not make an ongoing call choppy.","An IP conflict causes dropped connectivity, not varying delay."],3],["x54",0,5,"A technician wants to confirm whether a desktop PSU's output voltages are within specification. Which tool fits?",["A cable tester","A multimeter","A loopback plug","A toner probe"],1,"A multimeter (or a dedicated PSU tester) measures the actual output voltages. A cable tester checks network cable wiring, not power supply voltages.","Domain 5: Diagnostic tools",["A cable tester checks network cable wiring, not PSU voltages.",null,"A loopback plug tests a port's send and receive, not power output.","A toner probe traces cables and does not measure voltage."],1],["x55",0,5,"After a thunderstorm, a desktop shows no lights and no fan movement at all. The wall outlet powers a lamp fine. What should the technician do next?",["Reinstall the operating system","Reseat the memory modules","Test or replace the PSU","Update the BIOS firmware"],2,"With a working outlet and no signs of power at all, the PSU is the most likely failure after a surge. Reseating memory can fix POST problems, but the fans would still spin if power were present.","Domain 5: Power problems",["The OS cannot load on a PC with no power at all.","Reseating RAM helps with POST errors, but the fans would still spin with power.",null,"A BIOS update needs a working, powered system first."],2],["x56",0,5,"A PC restarts by itself overnight. The technician wants to see what the system recorded just before each restart. Which tool should they open?",["Device Manager","Disk Management","Task Manager","Event Viewer"],3,"Event Viewer keeps system logs with time stamps, including errors before unexpected shutdowns. Device Manager shows hardware and driver status but no history of events.","Domain 5: Diagnostic tools",["Device Manager shows hardware status but no history of events.","Disk Management handles partitions and volumes, not system event logs.","Task Manager shows what is running now, not what happened overnight.",null],1],["x57",0,5,"After a new sound card is installed, there is no audio and Device Manager shows a yellow exclamation mark on the card. What should the technician do?",["Install or update the card's driver","Replace the desk speakers","Enable Secure Boot in UEFI","Run CHKDSK on the system drive"],0,"A yellow exclamation mark in Device Manager usually means a missing or faulty driver. Replacing speakers would not clear a device error that Windows itself reports.","Domain 5: Diagnostic tools",[null,"The error is on the card in Device Manager, not the speakers.","Secure Boot checks boot loaders and does not cause a sound card driver error.","CHKDSK checks the file system, not device drivers."],2],["x58",0,5,"A new monitor shows one tiny black dot that stays in the same place no matter what image is displayed. What is this?",["Burn-in","A dead pixel","Wrong resolution","Backlight failure"],1,"A pixel that stays black on every image is a dead pixel. Burn-in is a faint ghost of a static image, not a single dark dot.","Domain 5: Display problems",["Burn-in is a faint ghost of a static image, not a single black dot.",null,"A wrong resolution makes the whole image fuzzy, not one dot.","A failed backlight dims the whole screen, not one pixel."],1],["x59",0,5,"An OLED phone shows a faint outline of the navigation bar on every screen, even while watching full-screen video. What is the likely cause?",["A group of dead pixels","A failing digitizer","Burn-in from a static image","A low refresh rate setting"],2,"OLED pixels age unevenly when a static element is shown for long periods, leaving a permanent ghost image. A failing digitizer affects touch input, not what the screen displays.","Domain 5: Display problems",["Dead pixels are dark dots, not the outline of an interface element.","A failing digitizer affects touch input, not the displayed image.",null,"Refresh rate affects motion smoothness, not permanent ghost images."],2],["x60",0,5,"A phone was dropped in water and still seems to work. What is the best first action?",["Charge it fully to check the battery","Keep using it normally while it dries in a bag of rice","Dry the inside with a hot hair dryer","Power it off at once and do not charge it"],3,"Powering off and not charging reduces the chance of short circuits while the device is dried and inspected. Charging a wet phone puts current through wet circuits and makes damage much more likely.","Domain 5: Mobile device problems",["Charging a wet phone sends current through wet circuits and risks shorts.","Using the phone while it dries keeps current flowing through wet parts.","High heat can damage the battery, screen and seals.",null],2],["x61",0,1,"A technician is about to replace a laptop's keyboard. Which step is MOST important before disconnecting any internal ribbon cables?",["Update the BIOS to the latest version","Disconnect the internal battery","Uninstall the keyboard driver","Remove the display assembly"],1,"Disconnecting the internal battery removes power from the board so cables and components are not damaged by a short. Updating the BIOS has nothing to do with safely removing hardware.","Objective 1.1: Laptop hardware",["A BIOS update is unrelated to physically swapping a keyboard and adds needless risk.",null,"Drivers are software; removing one does not protect the hardware during the repair.","The display usually stays in place for a keyboard swap and removing it adds work."],1],["x62",0,1,"A user wants a laptop display with true blacks because each pixel produces its own light and no backlight is needed. Which technology fits?",["IPS LCD","TN LCD","OLED","VA LCD"],2,"OLED pixels emit their own light, so they can turn off completely for true black. IPS, TN and VA are all LCD types that need a backlight behind the panel.","Objective 1.1: Display components",["IPS is an LCD panel type and always needs a backlight.","TN is an LCD panel type that relies on a backlight.",null,"VA panels have good contrast but are still backlit LCDs."],1],["x63",0,1,"While replacing a laptop's lid, a technician finds thin cables leading from the Wi-Fi card. Where are the wireless antennas in most laptops?",["Under the keyboard deck","Along the bottom panel beside the battery","Inside the power adapter","Around the display in the lid"],3,"Laptop Wi-Fi antennas usually run up into the display bezel, the highest point, for the best reception. Placing them under the keyboard would surround them with metal and weaken the signal.","Objective 1.1: Wireless antennas",["Under the keyboard the antennas would be shielded by metal parts and lose signal.","The battery area is low and crowded, which would block wireless reception.","The power adapter is outside the laptop and has no connection to the Wi-Fi card.",null],2],["x64",0,1,"A new employee wants work email, contacts and calendar from the company's Microsoft 365 account to sync to their Android phone. What should the technician do?",["Add the work email account in Settings","Turn on Bluetooth tethering to a PC","Enable NFC and tap the laptop","Connect a USB OTG adapter"],0,"Adding the Exchange or Microsoft 365 account on the phone syncs mail, contacts and calendar over the internet. Bluetooth tethering only shares a network connection and syncs no data.","Objective 1.3: Mobile device synchronization",[null,"Tethering shares the phone's internet connection; it does not sync mailbox data.","NFC is for short-range taps such as payments, not account synchronization.","A USB OTG adapter lets the phone use USB accessories, not sync cloud accounts."],2],["x65",0,1,"A salesperson turns on the phone's mobile hotspot to connect a laptop at a trade show. What should be configured so strangers nearby cannot join?",["Turn off GPS location services","Enable NFC payments","Set a strong WPA2 or WPA3 password","Turn on airplane mode after connecting the laptop"],2,"A hotspot is a small Wi-Fi network, so it needs strong wireless encryption and a password. Airplane mode would turn off the radios and stop the hotspot entirely.","Objective 1.3: Mobile hotspot",["GPS provides location and has no effect on who can join the hotspot.","NFC payments are unrelated to securing a Wi-Fi hotspot.",null,"Airplane mode disables the cellular connection, so the hotspot would stop working."],1],["x66",0,1,"A laptop's battery no longer holds a charge. When ordering a replacement, what matters MOST?",["Any battery with more cells, whatever the voltage","Matching voltage, connector and part number","A higher voltage than the original for faster charging","The same color as the laptop's case"],1,"A replacement must match the original's voltage, connector and part number to fit and work safely. A higher voltage battery can damage the laptop and is not a way to charge faster.","Objective 1.1: Battery replacement",["Cell count means little if the voltage or connector does not match the laptop.",null,"A higher voltage than specified can damage the laptop's circuits.","The color of the case has no bearing on whether a battery is compatible."],2],["x67",0,1,"A user with a new iPhone 16 asks which charging cable to buy. Which connector does the phone use?",["Lightning","Micro-USB","Mini-USB","USB-C"],3,"iPhones from the iPhone 15 onward use USB-C for charging and data. Lightning was Apple's earlier connector on previous iPhone models.","Objective 1.2: Mobile connection methods",["Lightning was used on earlier iPhones but was replaced by USB-C starting with the iPhone 15.","Micro-USB was common on older Android phones, never on iPhones.","Mini-USB is an older connector found on some cameras and devices, not iPhones.",null],1],["x68",0,1,"Two coworkers on the same carrier stand side by side. One phone shows 5G, while an older phone shows only LTE. What is the most likely reason?",["The older phone's modem does not support 5G","The carrier blocks older phones from its network towers","Wi-Fi calling is turned off","Bluetooth is interfering with cellular"],0,"A phone can only use cellular generations its modem supports, so an LTE-only phone never shows 5G. The carrier still serves older phones on LTE, so it is not blocking them.","Objective 1.3: Cellular networks",[null,"The older phone is connected on LTE, so the carrier is clearly not blocking it.","Wi-Fi calling affects calls over Wi-Fi, not which cellular generation is shown.","Bluetooth uses a different band and does not change the cellular generation."],2],["x69",0,2,"A help desk application looks up user information in the company directory using unencrypted LDAP. Which port must the firewall allow?",["443","3389","389","161"],2,"LDAP uses TCP port 389, and LDAPS uses 636. Port 3389 is RDP, which is for remote desktop, not directory queries.","Objective 2.1: Ports and protocols",["Port 443 is HTTPS web traffic, not directory lookups.","Port 3389 is Remote Desktop Protocol, not LDAP.",null,"Port 161 is SNMP for device monitoring, not directory services."],2],["x70",0,2,"A legacy file-transfer program opens its control connection on TCP port 21 and sends data unencrypted. Which protocol is it using?",["SFTP","TFTP","SMB","FTP"],3,"FTP uses port 21 for control and port 20 for data, with no encryption. SFTP runs over SSH on port 22 and encrypts the session.","Objective 2.1: Ports and protocols",["SFTP runs over SSH on port 22 and encrypts everything, unlike this program.","TFTP uses UDP port 69 and has no separate control connection on 21.","SMB uses port 445 for Windows file sharing, not port 21.",null],1],["x71",0,2,"Which of these applications should use TCP because every byte must arrive complete and in order?",["Live video streaming","A large file download","A VoIP phone call","A DNS name lookup"],1,"File downloads need guaranteed, ordered delivery, which TCP provides through acknowledgments and retransmission. Live video and voice prefer UDP because late packets are useless anyway.","Objective 2.1: TCP and UDP",["Live streaming usually favors UDP because speed matters more than resending lost frames.",null,"VoIP uses UDP since a retransmitted voice packet would arrive too late to help.","Most DNS lookups use UDP port 53 because they are small single exchanges."],2],["x72",0,2,"A large two-story home has Wi-Fi dead spots. The owner wants several units that pass traffic between themselves wirelessly and share one network name. What should be installed?",["A second router with its own DHCP","A PoE injector","A mesh Wi-Fi system","An unmanaged hub"],2,"Mesh Wi-Fi uses multiple nodes that link wirelessly and present one SSID for seamless coverage. A second router with its own DHCP creates a separate network and address conflicts.","Objective 2.3: Wireless networking",["A second router running DHCP creates a separate network and can cause address problems.","A PoE injector powers a device over Ethernet; it does not extend Wi-Fi coverage.",null,"A hub is a wired device and cannot fix wireless dead spots."],1],["x73",0,2,"Email sent to a company's domain is bouncing because senders cannot find where to deliver it. Which DNS record identifies the domain's mail server?",["MX","A","CNAME","TXT"],0,"The MX record names the mail server that accepts email for a domain. An A record maps a host name to an IPv4 address but does not mark it as the mail server.","Objective 2.6: DNS records",[null,"An A record maps a name to an IPv4 address but does not say where mail goes.","A CNAME creates an alias for another host name, not a mail route.","TXT records hold text such as SPF data, not the mail server location."],1],["x74",0,2,"Which DHCP setting controls how long a client may keep its assigned IP address before it must renew?",["Scope","Lease time","Reservation","Exclusion"],1,"The lease time sets how long an address is assigned before the client must renew it. The scope is the range of addresses the server can hand out.","Objective 2.6: DHCP",["The scope is the pool of addresses, not how long each one is kept.",null,"A reservation always gives one device the same address; it does not set duration.","An exclusion removes addresses from the pool so they are never handed out."],2],["x75",0,2,"A PC has the address 192.168.10.50 with subnet mask 255.255.255.0. Which of these addresses is on the same subnet?",["192.168.11.50","10.168.10.5","192.169.10.50","192.168.10.200"],3,"With a 255.255.255.0 mask the first three octets identify the network, so any 192.168.10.x host is local. 192.168.11.50 differs in the third octet, so it is on another subnet.","Objective 2.5: IPv4 addressing",["The third octet is 11 instead of 10, so it is a different subnet.","10.168.10.5 is in a completely different network range.","The second octet is 169, which puts it on another network entirely.",null],3],["x76",0,2,"Which wireless standard, also called Wi-Fi 5, operates only in the 5 GHz band?",["802.11n","802.11g","802.11ac","802.11b"],2,"802.11ac (Wi-Fi 5) uses only the 5 GHz band. 802.11n (Wi-Fi 4) can use both 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz.","Objective 2.3: Wireless standards",["802.11n is Wi-Fi 4 and works in both 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz.","802.11g is an older standard that uses only 2.4 GHz.",null,"802.11b is one of the oldest standards and uses only 2.4 GHz."],2],["x77",0,2,"A home user adds smart plugs and cameras and wants them kept away from the family laptops in case one is compromised. What should be configured on the SOHO router?",["Enable UPnP on the router","Put them on a guest or separate network","Point the router's DMZ setting at one of the smart plugs","Turn off the router's firewall"],1,"A guest or separate network isolates IoT devices from personal computers. UPnP lets devices open ports on their own, which increases exposure instead of isolating them.","Objective 2.4: SOHO network configuration",["UPnP lets devices open inbound ports automatically, which increases risk.",null,"A DMZ host is fully exposed to the internet, the opposite of protecting it.","Turning off the firewall removes protection for every device on the network."],2],["x78",0,2,"A rural office's only wired internet option runs over the existing copper telephone line. Which connection type is this?",["DSL","Cable","Fiber","Satellite"],0,"DSL carries internet data over ordinary telephone copper pairs. Cable internet uses coaxial cable from the TV provider instead.","Objective 2.7: Internet connection types",[null,"Cable internet uses coaxial cable from the cable TV network, not phone lines.","Fiber uses glass strands carrying light, not copper phone lines.","Satellite uses a dish and a radio link, not any telephone wiring."],1],["x79",0,2,"A city links the networks of its libraries, offices and fire stations spread across town. Which network type does this form?",["LAN","PAN","WLAN","MAN"],3,"A metropolitan area network spans a city or campus larger than a LAN but smaller than a WAN. A LAN covers a single building or site.","Objective 2.7: Network types",["A LAN covers one building or site, not many locations across a city.","A PAN links devices around one person, such as earbuds and a watch.","A WLAN is a wireless local network, usually within one site.",null],2],["x80",0,2,"Before placing a new access point, a technician wants to see which nearby networks use which channels so the least crowded one can be chosen. Which tool fits?",["Wi-Fi analyzer","Toner probe","Cable tester","Loopback plug"],0,"A Wi-Fi analyzer shows nearby networks, their channels and signal strength. A cable tester only checks wired cable pairs.","Objective 2.8: Networking tools",[null,"A toner probe traces wired cables and cannot see wireless channels.","A cable tester checks wiring continuity, not wireless channel use.","A loopback plug tests a network port, not the wireless environment."],1],["x81",0,2,"The security team wants an exact copy of all traffic crossing the internet link sent to a monitoring appliance, without adding a device that could drop packets if it fails. What should be installed?",["Patch panel","Network tap","Punchdown block","PoE injector"],1,"A network tap passively copies traffic from a link to a monitoring port. A patch panel only organizes cable terminations and copies nothing.","Objective 2.8: Networking tools",["A patch panel organizes cable runs but does not copy any traffic.",null,"A punchdown block terminates wiring and has no monitoring function.","A PoE injector adds power to a cable and does not copy traffic."],3],["x82",0,2,"A small business wants one appliance at its internet edge that combines firewall, intrusion prevention, antivirus scanning and web content filtering. Which device fits?",["Load balancer","Managed switch","Syslog server","Unified threat management appliance"],3,"A UTM appliance bundles several security functions into one box, which suits small networks. A load balancer spreads traffic across servers and does no threat filtering.","Objective 2.4: Networked hosts",["A load balancer distributes incoming requests across servers; it does not scan for threats.","A managed switch forwards frames and offers VLANs, not antivirus or content filtering.","A syslog server collects log messages but does not filter traffic.",null],3],["x83",0,3,"A technician connects a cable modem to the coaxial wall outlet. Which screw-on connector is used on that coax cable?",["RJ11","BNC","F-type","LC"],2,"F-type is the threaded coax connector used for cable modems and TV. BNC is a bayonet-style coax connector found mostly on older networks and video equipment.","Objective 3.1: Cables and connectors",["RJ11 is a telephone connector, not a coax connector.","BNC twists and locks rather than screwing on, and is not used for cable modems.",null,"LC is a fiber optic connector, not coax."],2],["x84",0,3,"A data closet needs a small form factor fiber connector with a push-and-latch design, often paired side by side for transmit and receive. Which connector fits?",["ST","SC","F-type","LC"],3,"LC is a small form factor fiber connector with a latch, commonly used in duplex pairs on switches. SC is also push-pull but is larger, so it takes more space.","Objective 3.1: Cables and connectors",["ST uses a twist-lock bayonet rather than a small latching body.","SC is push-pull but noticeably larger than a small form factor connector.","F-type is a threaded coax connector, not a fiber connector.",null],3],["x85",0,3,"A new laptop has Thunderbolt 4 ports. Which physical connector do these ports use?",["USB-C","Mini DisplayPort","Lightning","Micro-USB"],0,"Thunderbolt 3 and 4 use the USB-C connector shape. Mini DisplayPort was the connector for the older Thunderbolt 1 and 2.","Objective 3.1: Peripheral cables",[null,"Mini DisplayPort was used by Thunderbolt 1 and 2, not Thunderbolt 4.","Lightning is an Apple phone connector and does not carry Thunderbolt.","Micro-USB is a small legacy USB connector with no Thunderbolt support."],2],["x86",0,3,"A video editor wants the fastest possible scratch volume from two identical SSDs, keeps project backups elsewhere and does not need redundancy. Which RAID level fits?",["RAID 1","RAID 0","RAID 5","RAID 6"],1,"RAID 0 stripes data across both drives for speed and full capacity but offers no fault tolerance. RAID 1 mirrors the drives, which gives redundancy but no write speed gain.","Objective 3.3: RAID",["RAID 1 mirrors data for redundancy, which the editor said is not needed.",null,"RAID 5 needs at least three drives and adds parity overhead.","RAID 6 needs at least four drives and uses two drives' worth of parity."],2],["x87",0,3,"A user buys a full-size graphics card for a desktop. Which motherboard slot should it be installed in?",["PCIe x1","M.2 E key","PCIe x16","A conventional PCI slot"],2,"Graphics cards are designed for PCIe x16 slots, which provide the most lanes and bandwidth. A PCIe x1 slot is too narrow for a full-size card's connector.","Objective 3.4: Motherboards and expansion",["A PCIe x1 slot has far fewer lanes and is too short for a graphics card's connector.","M.2 E key slots are for small wireless cards, not graphics cards.",null,"Conventional PCI is obsolete and is not compatible with modern graphics cards."],1],["x88",0,3,"What does enabling Secure Boot in UEFI firmware do?",["Encrypts the whole system drive","Shortens POST by skipping the memory and hardware tests","Updates firmware automatically","Blocks unsigned boot loaders from starting"],3,"Secure Boot only lets boot loaders and OS components signed by trusted keys run, which blocks boot-level malware. Drive encryption is done by tools like BitLocker, not by Secure Boot.","Objective 3.4: BIOS/UEFI settings",["Drive encryption comes from tools such as BitLocker, not from Secure Boot.","Fast boot options shorten POST; Secure Boot checks signatures instead.","Firmware updates are a separate process that Secure Boot does not perform.",null],2],["x89",0,3,"A technician is installing a CPU into an LGA socket and must take care not to bend any pins. Where are the pins on an LGA platform?",["On the underside of the CPU","In the motherboard socket","On both the CPU and the socket","There are none; the CPU is soldered"],1,"In a land grid array design, the pins are in the motherboard socket and the CPU has flat contact pads. PGA designs are the opposite, with pins on the CPU.","Objective 3.4: CPU sockets",["Pins on the CPU describe a PGA design, not LGA.",null,"Only one side has pins; the other side has flat pads or holes.","Soldered CPUs are BGA designs found in laptops, not socketed LGA chips."],3],["x90",0,3,"A new build's components draw about 520 watts at full load. Which power supply is the BEST choice?",["450 W","500 W","650 W","1600 W"],2,"A 650 W unit covers the 520 W load with sensible headroom for peaks and efficiency. A 1600 W unit is far oversized and wastes money and efficiency at this load.","Objective 3.5: Power supplies",["A 450 W supply is below the 520 W load and would shut down or fail.","A 500 W supply is still under the full load, with no headroom.",null,"A 1600 W supply is heavily oversized for a 520 W build."],3],["x91",0,3,"After building a PC, the technician finds the system will not start. The 24-pin connector is attached, but a second power cable near the CPU socket is not. Which connector is missing?",["8-pin EPS12V","SATA power","Molex","6-pin PCIe"],0,"The 8-pin EPS12V connector near the socket feeds the CPU's voltage regulators. SATA power connectors go to drives, not the motherboard.","Objective 3.5: Power connectors",[null,"SATA power connectors supply storage drives, not the CPU area of the board.","Molex is an older peripheral power connector, not the CPU power input.","A 6-pin PCIe connector powers graphics cards, not the CPU."],2],["x92",0,3,"In the laser printing process, which step uses the laser to write the page image onto the charged imaging drum?",["Charging","Developing","Fusing","Exposing"],3,"During exposing, the laser discharges spots on the drum to form the image. Developing comes next, when toner sticks to those discharged areas.","Objective 3.7: Laser printers",["Charging puts a uniform charge on the drum before any image is drawn.","Developing applies toner to the image the laser already drew.","Fusing melts toner onto the paper near the end of the process.",null],2],["x93",0,3,"An office worker needs to scan a 40-page contract without placing each page on the glass by hand. Which multifunction printer feature helps?",["Flatbed glass","Duplexing assembly","Automatic document feeder","Fuser assembly"],2,"An automatic document feeder pulls a stack of pages through the scanner one at a time. The flatbed glass requires placing each page manually.","Objective 3.6: Multifunction devices",["The flatbed requires placing each page by hand, which the worker wants to avoid.","A duplexing assembly prints on both sides; it does not feed originals for scanning.",null,"The fuser melts toner onto printed pages and has no role in scanning."],1],["x94",0,3,"A warehouse dot matrix printer produces text that gets lighter every week. Which consumable should be replaced?",["Toner cartridge","Ribbon","Fuser","Imaging drum"],1,"Impact printers strike an inked ribbon against the paper, and the ribbon fades with use. Toner and imaging drums belong to laser printers.","Objective 3.7: Impact printers",["Toner is used by laser printers, not dot matrix printers.",null,"A fuser is a laser printer part that bonds toner with heat.","An imaging drum is part of a laser printer, not an impact printer."],1],["x95",0,3,"A technician needs to control two tower PCs from a single keyboard, mouse and monitor on a small desk. Which device fits?",["KVM switch","USB hub","Docking station","Port replicator"],0,"A KVM switch shares one keyboard, video display and mouse between several computers. A USB hub adds ports to one computer but cannot switch between PCs.","Objective 3.1: Peripheral devices",[null,"A USB hub adds ports to one computer but cannot switch between two.","A docking station connects one laptop to peripherals, not two desktops.","A port replicator extends one laptop's ports and does not switch computers."],1],["x96",0,3,"A server has four 2 TB drives configured as RAID 5. About how much usable storage does the array provide?",["4 TB","8 TB","2 TB","6 TB"],3,"RAID 5 uses one drive's worth of space for parity, so usable space is (4 - 1) x 2 TB = 6 TB. 8 TB would be the raw total with no parity at all.","Objective 3.3: RAID",["4 TB would be the result with two drives of overhead, as in RAID 10 or RAID 6.","8 TB is the raw total and ignores the parity space RAID 5 needs.","2 TB is the size of a single drive, far less than the array provides.",null],3],["x97",0,3,"A technician is buying an M.2 NVMe SSD that runs on four PCIe lanes. Which keying notch does such a drive typically use?",["A key","E key","M key","No keying notch"],2,"M key M.2 slots and drives support up to four PCIe lanes, which NVMe SSDs use. E key is used for Wi-Fi and Bluetooth cards.","Objective 3.3: Storage devices",["A key M.2 slots are used for wireless cards, not NVMe storage.","E key is common for Wi-Fi and Bluetooth modules, not x4 NVMe drives.",null,"Every M.2 card has a keying notch to prevent wrong insertion."],3],["x98",0,4,"A company moves to an office suite that staff use through a web browser, while the provider manages the servers, software and updates. Which cloud service model is this?",["SaaS","IaaS","PaaS","DaaS"],0,"Software as a Service delivers a finished application the customer simply uses. In IaaS the customer would manage operating systems and applications on rented servers.","Objective 4.2: Cloud service models",[null,"IaaS provides virtual servers the customer must manage, not a ready-made office suite.","PaaS gives developers a platform to run their own code, not finished office apps.","Desktop as a Service streams whole desktops rather than a browser-based app suite."],1],["x99",0,4,"A company builds cloud infrastructure in its own data center that only its own departments can use. Which deployment model is this?",["Public","Community","Hybrid","Private"],3,"A private cloud is used by a single organization, whether hosted on-site or by a provider. A community cloud is shared by several organizations with common needs.","Objective 4.2: Cloud deployment models",["A public cloud is shared by many unrelated customers of a provider.","A community cloud is shared by several organizations with common requirements.","A hybrid cloud combines private and public clouds, not only an internal one.",null],1],["x100",0,4,"Workloads from many different customers run on the same physical provider hardware but are kept logically separated from each other. Which cloud characteristic is this?",["Rapid elasticity","Multitenancy","Metered utilization","High availability"],1,"Multitenancy means several customers share the same underlying resources while staying isolated. Rapid elasticity is about scaling resources up and down quickly.","Objective 4.2: Cloud characteristics",["Rapid elasticity describes scaling quickly, not sharing hardware among customers.",null,"Metered utilization is paying for what is used, not sharing hardware.","High availability is about keeping services running through failures."],2],["x101",0,4,"A company wants its cloud-hosted web app to keep running even if one of the provider's data centers goes offline. What is the BEST design choice?",["Run instances in two or more zones","Give the single VM more RAM","Take a snapshot of the VM once a day to cloud storage","Move to a bigger single VM"],0,"Spreading instances across availability zones lets the app survive the loss of one data center. A bigger single VM is still one point of failure in one location.","Objective 4.2: Cloud high availability",[null,"More RAM improves performance but the single VM still fails with its data center.","Daily snapshots help recovery later but do not keep the app running during an outage.","A bigger VM is still a single instance in one location."],2],["x102",0,4,"Users want files they save on their work laptop to show up automatically on their phone and in a web browser. What should the technician set up?",["VDI","A Type 1 hypervisor","A cloud file sync service","Port forwarding"],2,"A cloud file synchronization service keeps the same files updated across devices. VDI streams a hosted desktop rather than syncing files to each device.","Objective 4.2: Cloud file storage",["VDI streams a remote desktop instead of keeping local files in sync.","A Type 1 hypervisor runs virtual machines and does not sync user files.",null,"Port forwarding exposes an internal service and does not synchronize files."],1],["x103",0,4,"A technician creates a 200 GB thin-provisioned virtual disk for a new VM that currently holds 30 GB of data. How much space does it use on the host?",["All 200 GB is reserved at once","About 30 GB, growing as data is written","None until the VM is shut down for the first time","Exactly half of the 200 GB"],1,"A thin-provisioned disk only consumes host storage as the guest writes data, up to its maximum size. A thick-provisioned disk would reserve the full 200 GB immediately.","Objective 4.1: Virtualization",["Reserving the full size at creation describes thick provisioning, not thin.",null,"The disk uses host space as data is written, whether or not the VM is running.","Thin provisioning has no fixed half allocation; it grows with actual data."],3],["x104",0,4,"A developer needs to run and test an app built for ARM-based phones on an x86 Windows PC. What is needed?",["A Type 1 hypervisor","A container","Virtual desktop infrastructure","An emulator"],3,"An emulator imitates a different processor architecture in software, so ARM code can run on x86 hardware. A hypervisor or container shares the host's CPU architecture and cannot run ARM code natively.","Objective 4.1: Emulation and virtualization",["A hypervisor runs guests on the same CPU architecture as the host, so ARM code will not run.","Containers share the host kernel and CPU architecture and cannot run ARM binaries on x86.","VDI streams hosted desktops and does not translate processor instructions.",null],3],["x105",0,5,"A technician has tested a theory and confirmed the cause of a problem. According to the CompTIA troubleshooting methodology, what should happen NEXT?",["Document findings and close the ticket","Plan and implement the solution","Question the user again for details","Verify full system functionality"],1,"After the theory is confirmed, the next step is to establish a plan of action and implement the solution. Verifying full functionality comes after the fix is in place.","Objective 5.1: Troubleshooting methodology",["Documentation is the final step, after the fix is applied and verified.",null,"Questioning the user belongs to identifying the problem, which is already done.","Verification comes after the solution has been implemented."],3],["x106",0,5,"A laptop's picture flickers or goes dark only when the lid is moved to certain angles. What is the most likely cause?",["A loose or damaged display cable","A failing storage drive","The wrong keyboard layout","Too little system memory"],0,"The display cable runs through the hinge, so wear or a loose connection there causes problems that change with lid position. Memory or storage faults would not depend on the lid angle.","Objective 5.2: Display issues",[null,"A failing drive causes slowness or boot errors, not flicker tied to lid movement.","A keyboard layout setting has no effect on the display.","Low memory slows the system but does not change with the lid angle."],2],["x107",0,5,"A laptop's fan runs loudly all the time and the bottom of the case gets very hot, even with light use. What should the technician try first?",["Replace the display panel","Reinstall the web browser and clear its cache","Clean dust out of the vents and fan","Add an external monitor"],2,"Dust blocking vents and fans traps heat and makes the fan run at full speed. Replacing the display does nothing for cooling.","Objective 5.4: Overheating",["The display panel has nothing to do with the fan noise or heat.","Reinstalling a browser does not clear a hardware cooling problem.",null,"An external monitor adds load rather than fixing the airflow."],1],["x108",0,5,"After a graphics card is installed, the PC's fans and lights come on, but the monitor shows No signal. The monitor cable is still plugged into the motherboard's video port. What should the technician do?",["Replace the CMOS battery","Reinstall the operating system and the graphics drivers","Change the DNS servers","Move the cable to the new card's port"],3,"When a dedicated graphics card is installed, onboard video is often disabled, so the monitor must connect to the card. Reinstalling the OS will not help when no video reaches the screen.","Objective 5.2: Video troubleshooting",["The CMOS battery keeps settings and time; it does not route video.","Reinstalling the OS cannot fix a monitor plugged into a disabled port.","DNS affects name resolution on the network, not the video output.",null],2],["x109",0,5,"One of the two drives in a RAID 0 array fails completely. What happens to the data on the array?",["The array keeps running in a degraded state","All data on the array is lost","Parity rebuilds the missing data","Nothing, because RAID 0 mirrors data"],1,"RAID 0 stripes data with no redundancy, so losing any drive destroys the whole array. A degraded but running state applies to fault-tolerant levels such as RAID 1 or RAID 5.","Objective 5.3: Storage troubleshooting",["Only fault-tolerant RAID levels can keep running degraded after a drive loss.",null,"RAID 0 has no parity, so there is nothing to rebuild from.","RAID 1 mirrors data; RAID 0 only stripes it."],3],["x110",0,5,"An older monitor connected with a VGA cable shows everything with a strong pink tint, as if one color is missing. What should the technician check first?",["The video cable and its connector pins","The monitor's refresh rate setting in the display options","The panel for a single dead pixel","The screen for burn-in"],0,"Analog VGA sends red, green and blue on separate pins, so a loose cable or bent pin can drop one color and tint the whole image. A dead pixel affects only one tiny spot.","Objective 5.2: Display issues",[null,"A wrong refresh rate causes flicker or no signal, not a missing color channel.","A dead pixel is one small spot, not a tint across the whole screen.","Burn-in leaves a faint ghost image, not a color cast on everything."],3],["x111",0,5,"A tablet's screen no longer switches between portrait and landscape when the user turns it. What should the technician check first?",["Whether the digitizer failed","Whether the battery is low","Whether rotation lock is on","Whether GPS is disabled"],2,"Rotation lock stops the screen from turning and is a quick setting users often enable by accident. A failed digitizer would affect touch input, not rotation.","Objective 5.5: Mobile device issues",["A digitizer handles touch input and does not control screen rotation.","A low battery does not stop the screen from rotating.",null,"GPS provides location and has nothing to do with screen orientation."],1],["x112",0,5,"A PC with a static IP can reach file servers on its own subnet but cannot reach any internet site, even by IP address. Other PCs work fine. Which setting is most likely wrong?",["Default gateway","DNS server","Wi-Fi channel","MAC address"],0,"The default gateway is the router used for traffic leaving the local subnet, so a wrong gateway breaks all remote access while local access works. A DNS problem would still allow reaching sites by IP address.","Objective 5.6: Network troubleshooting",[null,"A DNS problem breaks names but still allows reaching sites by IP address.","The PC reaches local servers, so its wireless channel is working.","MAC addresses are burned into the NIC and are not a setting in a static IP configuration."],3],["x113",0,5,"A network cable runs across the top of fluorescent light fixtures, and the switch port shows many errors and slow speeds. What is the BEST fix?",["Change the PC's IP address","Reroute it or use shielded cable","Update the web browser","Replace the user's monitor and keyboard"],1,"Fluorescent ballasts create electromagnetic interference, so moving the cable away or using shielded cable stops the errors. Changing the IP address does nothing for signal quality.","Objective 5.6: Network troubleshooting",["An IP change does not affect electrical interference on the cable.",null,"A browser update cannot fix physical errors on the cable.","The monitor is unrelated to errors on the network link."],2],["x114",0,5,"A desktop has no network access, and the link light on its network port is off. What should the technician check first?",["Reinstall the operating system","Change the DNS server","Check the patch cable and switch port","Flush the ARP cache"],2,"No link light points to a physical layer problem, so the cable and switch port are the first things to check. DNS settings cannot affect whether a link light comes on.","Objective 5.6: Network troubleshooting",["Reinstalling the OS is drastic and does not fix a physical link problem.","DNS works above the physical layer and cannot turn a link light on.",null,"The ARP cache is a software table and does not affect the physical link."],1],["x115",0,5,"A laser printer often pulls several sheets at once or grabs no paper at all. Which part is most likely worn?",["Fuser assembly","Toner cartridge","Transfer belt and its cleaning blade","Pickup and separation rollers"],3,"Worn pickup and separation rollers lose grip, causing misfeeds and multiple sheets. A failing fuser causes smeared toner, not feeding problems.","Objective 5.7: Printer troubleshooting",["A failing fuser leaves toner that smears; it does not feed paper.","The toner cartridge affects print quality, not how sheets are picked up.","The transfer belt moves toner to paper after the sheet is already inside.",null],2],["x116",0,5,"Every page from a laser printer has a thin vertical black line in exactly the same place. What is the most likely cause?",["A scratched or dirty imaging drum","The wrong print driver","Low printer memory","A faulty duplexing unit in the rear panel"],0,"A scratch or debris on the drum marks every page at the same spot as it turns. A wrong driver usually prints garbled characters, not a single clean line.","Objective 5.7: Printer troubleshooting",[null,"A wrong driver typically produces garbled text, not one repeating line.","Low memory causes incomplete or failed jobs, not a vertical line.","The duplexing unit flips paper and does not mark the page with toner."],3],["x117",0,5,"Pages from a laser printer are evenly faded across the whole sheet, but otherwise look normal. What is the most likely cause?",["A dirty pickup roller","The wrong paper tray","Low toner","A loose network cable"],2,"Evenly light printing usually means the toner is running low. A dirty pickup roller causes feeding problems, not faded print.","Objective 5.7: Printer troubleshooting",["A pickup roller affects paper feeding, not how dark the print is.","Using a different tray changes the paper source, not print density.",null,"A loose network cable stops jobs from arriving; it does not fade them."],1],["x118",0,5,"A user installed two 8 GB memory modules, but the system reports only 8 GB of RAM. What is the most likely cause?",["The OS is a 64-bit edition","One module is unseated or failed","The CPU runs too fast","The storage drive is full"],1,"When only half the memory shows, one module is usually not fully seated or is defective. A 64-bit OS supports far more than 16 GB, so it is not the limit.","Objective 5.2: Memory troubleshooting",["A 64-bit OS can use far more than 16 GB, so it would not hide a module.",null,"CPU speed does not reduce the amount of RAM the system detects.","A full drive affects free space, not the RAM the system reports."],3],["x119",0,5,"After a new motherboard is installed, the PC powers on for a second and immediately shuts off. The power supply tests fine. What should the technician check?",["Whether the OS is corrupted","Whether the monitor cable is loose at the graphics card","Whether the DNS setting is wrong","Whether a standoff is shorting the board"],3,"A misplaced standoff touching the back of the board can short it and trigger instant shutdown. The OS never loads in this case, so it cannot be the cause.","Objective 5.2: Power and boot issues",["The system shuts off before the OS even loads, so the OS is not the cause.","A loose monitor cable causes no picture but does not make the PC shut off.","DNS settings have no effect on whether a PC stays powered on.",null],3],["x120",0,5,"A USB-C laptop charges very slowly and shows a warning about a low-power charger. The user borrowed a small phone charger. What is the most likely cause?",["The charger supplies too few watts","The battery is swollen","The laptop's display cable is loose inside the hinge","The OS needs reinstalling"],0,"USB-C Power Delivery negotiates wattage, and a small phone charger cannot supply what a laptop needs. A swollen battery is a physical fault and would not trigger a low-power charger warning.","Objective 5.5: Mobile power issues",[null,"A swollen battery shows as physical bulging, not a low-power charger warning.","The display cable affects the screen, not charging speed.","Reinstalling the OS will not change how much power a charger can provide."],2]]);
